{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794543681225,"sessionID":"ses_e2cdd3a02ffe7tBn2LFIz7jxoD","part":{"partID":"prt_1d3237c12001iXyeShheDGyqaZ","sessionID":"ses_e2cdd3a02ffe7tBn2LFIz7jxoD","messageID":"msg_1d32368fe001BUxSiUjJmca3rM","type":"tool","id":"call_661d23fead6e44fda6f284a0","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/REPO RULES.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/REPO RULES.md, lines 1-108\n1: # REPO RULES\n2: \n3: Repo-local operating rules. `AGENTS.md` **Gate 5 (§2)** makes reading this mandatory\n4: before your first write of the session; this document then routes you to every rule\n5: file that governs what you are about to do. **It is a router, not a rulebook:** it\n6: holds no rules of its own.\n7: \n8: ---\n9: \n10: ## 1. HOW TO USE THIS\n11: \n12: 1. **Match on the action you are about to take**, not the topic of the request.\n13: 2. **Load before the action.** A rule read afterwards is a post-mortem.\n14: 3. **A file already in context is not re-read.**\n15: 4. **Every trigger that fires is loaded, not just the first.** They compose; the more\n16:    specific wins on conflict. Three and four firing at once is the normal case, not an\n17:    edge case.\n18: 5. **Nothing fires → `AGENTS.md` alone governs.** Do not hunt for a rule to apply.\n19: \n20: ### Precedence\n21: \n22: | Level | Source | Can be overridden? |\n23: |-------|--------|--------------------|\n24: | 1 | Every `AGENTS.md` §1 hard blocker, the Four Laws, PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK, Comment Hygiene, and every mandatory gate in §2 | No |\n25: | 2 | An explicit, in-the-moment operator instruction | Not applicable, it is the instruction |\n26: | 3 | These rule files, and the guidance in a skill's `SKILL.md` | Only by level 1 or 2 |\n27: | 4 | General judgment | By anything above |\n28: \n29: A rule file may tighten `AGENTS.md`. None relaxes a HARD BLOCK or authorizes what\n30: `AGENTS.md` forbids. Gate 5 does not change that: it makes the **load** mandatory,\n31: while what you load stays at level 3, the obligation to read is tier 1, the content\n32: is not.\n33: \n34: ---\n35: \n36: ## 2. TRIGGER TABLE\n37: \n38: | You are about to… | Load | It settles |\n39: |-------------------|------|-----------|\n40: | Add a file, module, class, interface, option, layer, or dependency · write a spec or plan that adds one · generalize something that works · write \"flexible\", \"future-proof\", \"scalable\", \"extensible\", \"might need\", \"best practice\", \"while we're here\" · add a test beyond the coverage floor | [`prevent-overengineering.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md) | Whether this should exist at all, and at what size |\n41: | Touch a file outside the ask · fix something noticed in passing · change something else because it would make the fix easier · rename, reformat, or delete beyond the named area · deviate from an approved plan · decide what to do with the rest when part of the work is blocked · notice a defect, smell or stale comment outside the files in scope | [`scope-discipline.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md) | What is yours to change, and how to raise what isn't |\n42: | Say \"done\", \"works\", \"fixed\", \"passing\", \"no regressions\" · report a result · quote a number · act on a tool's or sub-agent's success report · close out a turn · report a file path or command output · write a completion summary or tick something off as done | [`evidence-and-proof.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md) | What counts as proof, how a green run lies, what an honest close-out contains |\n43: | Decide whether to hand work to another runtime at all · hand it to a CLI executor, sub-agent, fan-out lineage, or deep loop · compose the prompt one will act on · accept or quote what one returned · answer a judgment question from your own reading alone | [`delegation-and-orchestration.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/delegation-and-orchestration.md) | The orchestrating posture, what a brief must carry, why one model is one opinion |\n44: | Delete, overwrite, migrate, deploy, publish, send, install · force-push or rewrite history · change a shared contract · touch auth, data, or config · make any call that leaves this machine · truncate · touch branches, tags, or reflogs | [`blast-radius.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/blast-radius.md) | Reversibility, the rollback sentence, when to stop for a yes |\n45: | Diagnose a failure · make a red check green · attempt the same fix twice · add a special case, retry, sleep, or broadened catch · call a failure a flake, infra, or pre-existing | [`root-cause-and-debugging.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/root-cause-and-debugging.md) | Fixing the producer instead of the symptom, and when to level up to the seam |\n46: | Answer without certainty · contradict the operator · fill a gap with a plausible guess · hit a contradiction between two things that must both be true · name a path, flag, function, version, or number you have not verified · not know while a plausible answer is available · see sources disagree, or the code contradict the spec, the docs or the operator · the operator asserts something you believe is wrong · change a checked answer after pushback or doubt, or drop an approach before it concludes · cite a rule, restriction, policy, limit or permission, or decline a request | [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/uncertainty-and-honesty.md) | Confidence bands, UNKNOWN, contradiction halts |\n47: | Write any substantive reply, an answer, an explanation, a close-out or a status · draft or edit any sentence a reader will read · present a recommendation, a fork, or a trade-off · start a multi-step stretch of work the reader will not see inside · report what a long autonomous run found · the reader says they did not follow or asks for a plainer version | [`communication.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md) | How a reply reads: answer first, one recommended path, length, filler, plain sentences, punctuation, and no table in a reply |\n48: | End a turn · report work done, blocked, or partly done · end a turn while something you started is still running · ask the operator anything · end a turn on a fork or two acceptable paths · continue past a decision nobody made | [`communication-handoff.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md) | What is the operator's to do next, what is still running and what resumes it, and when a question becomes a structured choice |\n49: | Wire, rewire or remove a mode in a parent-hub skill · edit a hub's registry, router, root `ROUTER.md`, `graph-metadata.json` or `SKILL.md` mode table, or a mode packet's own `ROUTER.md` · report that a mode is registered, routed, reachable or integrated · quote a per-hub gate result · create a new skill under a hub, or refactor one that already lives there | [`skill-hub-routing.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/skill-hub-routing.md) | The two routing stages, what \"integrated\" requires, and checking the hub you actually changed |\n50: \n51: ---\n52: \n53: ## 3. INDEX\n54: \n55: | Rule | Summary |\n56: |------|---------|\n57: | [Overengineering](.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md) | Build the smallest thing that solves the stated problem; take a costlier move only by naming what fails at the cheaper one. |\n58: | [Scope discipline](.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md) | The requested scope is the deliverable. Adjacent problems get named, not fixed. |\n59: | [Evidence and proof](.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md) | A claim is only as strong as the observation behind it, and a receipt confirms only what could have contradicted it. Distinguish observed from derived from inferred. |\n60: | [Delegation and orchestration](.skilled/repo-rules/delegation-and-orchestration.md) | Delegating makes you the orchestrator; brief with evidence, and no single model's verdict closes a question. |\n61: | [Blast radius](.skilled/repo-rules/blast-radius.md) | Size effort to what the change can break; no irreversible step without a named rollback and a yes. |\n62: | [Root cause](.skilled/repo-rules/root-cause-and-debugging.md) | Fix the producer, not the symptom; every fix names the mechanism that caused the failure. |\n63: | [Uncertainty and honesty](.skilled/repo-rules/uncertainty-and-honesty.md) | Never fabricate; mark the confidence you actually have and halt on a contradiction. |\n64: | [Communication](.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md) | Write so the reader can act after one pass: no table in a reply, nothing that does not carry information. |\n65: | [Communication handoff](.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md) | End every turn by naming what is now the operator's to do, in the form that lets them do it. |\n66: | [Hub routing](.skilled/repo-rules/skill-hub-routing.md) | A hub routes in two stages, so a mode registered on one surface is not reachable, and a gate run without its hub argument reports on a hub you did not touch. |\n67: \n68: Each file expands `AGENTS.md` and is bounded by it: where a rule file appears to\n69: permit something `AGENTS.md` restricts, `AGENTS.md` wins and the rule file is wrong.\n70: \n71: ---\n72: \n73: ## 4. SCOPE OF THIS DOCUMENT\n74: \n75: **In:** how to think and act, restraint, scope, evidence, risk, diagnosis, honesty,\n76: the posture to hold when work is handed to another runtime, how the resulting reply\n77: reads, what you may claim about wiring you have changed, and how a turn hands control\n78: back to the operator. The delegation posture was the first widening and Delivery the\n79: second, added when `AGENTS.md` §8 moved down. Delivery is one of two rules here whose\n80: trigger is a whole turn rather than a specific action, the other being the handback rule,\n81: which fires at every turn's end, and `AGENTS.md` §4, under Reply Rules and Mandates, keeps\n82: the two clauses that must bind even when nothing loads.\n83: \n84: **Out:** skill routing, workflow selection, spec-folder mechanics, and the *mechanics*\n85: of agent and CLI dispatch: which agent, which command, which model, which flags.\n86: Those belong to `AGENTS.md` §2 and the skills it routes to, and are deliberately\n87: absent here so each has exactly one place to change. The line is between plumbing and\n88: posture: how to dispatch is theirs, how to think while dispatching is ours.\n89: \n90: **The routing carve-out, added deliberately as the third widening.** *Selecting* a\n91: route stays Out. *Verifying wiring you changed, and what you may claim about it*, is\n92: In; it is an evidence obligation whose subject happens to be routing. `skill-hub-routing.md`\n93: is the one rule on this side of that line, and it carries no route-selection guidance:\n94: the mechanics stay in the skills, and the rule points at them. A fourth widening that\n95: admits selection itself would dissolve the boundary; this one does not.\n96: \n97: **The ask-surface carve-out, added as the fourth widening, on an operator decision that\n98: overrode a research refusal.** A four-iteration decision-test run returned *refuse* here:\n99: naming a question tool per runtime reads as the \"which command\" family, and the paragraph\n100: above pre-refuses a fourth widening that admits selection. The operator widened it anyway,\n101: and what follows is the narrowing that keeps the boundary standing. *Choosing between\n102: runtimes, agents, commands, models or flags* stays Out, unchanged. *Naming the surface that\n103: asks the operator a question, in the runtime you are already running in*, is In. That is not\n104: a selection, because nothing is being selected: the runtime is a given, and the rule records\n105: which of its surfaces carries an obligation the rule already imposes. `communication-handoff.md`\n106: is the one rule on this side of that line, its §5 is the only place a tool name appears, and\n107: it names only what this repository confirms. A fifth widening that let a rule pick between\n108: runtimes would be the dissolution this one avoids.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/REPO RULES.md, lines 1-108\n1: # REPO RULES\n2: \n3: Repo-local operating rules. `AGENTS.md` **Gate 5 (§2)** makes reading this mandatory\n4: before your first write of the session; this document then routes you to every rule\n5: file that governs what you are about to do. **It is a router, not a rulebook:** it\n6: holds no rules of its own.\n7: \n8: ---\n9: \n10: ## 1. HOW TO USE THIS\n11: \n12: 1. **Match on the action you are about to take**, not the topic of the request.\n13: 2. **Load before the action.** A rule read afterwards is a post-mortem.\n14: 3. **A file already in context is not re-read.**\n15: 4. **Every trigger that fires is loaded, not just the first.** They compose; the more\n16:    specific wins on conflict. Three and four firing at once is the normal case, not an\n17:    edge case.\n18: 5. **Nothing fires → `AGENTS.md` alone governs.** Do not hunt for a rule to apply.\n19: \n20: ### Precedence\n21: \n22: | Level | Source | Can be overridden? |\n23: |-------|--------|--------------------|\n24: | 1 | Every `AGENTS.md` §1 hard blocker, the Four Laws, PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK, Comment Hygiene, and every mandatory gate in §2 | No |\n25: | 2 | An explicit, in-the-moment operator instruction | Not applicable, it is the instruction |\n26: | 3 | These rule files, and the guidance in a skill's `SKILL.md` | Only by level 1 or 2 |\n27: | 4 | General judgment | By anything above |\n28: \n29: A rule file may tighten `AGENTS.md`. None relaxes a HARD BLOCK or authorizes what\n30: `AGENTS.md` forbids. Gate 5 does not change that: it makes the **load** mandatory,\n31: while what you load stays at level 3, the obligation to read is tier 1, the content\n32: is not.\n33: \n34: ---\n35: \n36: ## 2. TRIGGER TABLE\n37: \n38: | You are about to… | Load | It settles |\n39: |-------------------|------|-----------|\n40: | Add a file, module, class, interface, option, layer, or dependency · write a spec or plan that adds one · generalize something that works · write \"flexible\", \"future-proof\", \"scalable\", \"extensible\", \"might need\", \"best practice\", \"while we're here\" · add a test beyond the coverage floor | [`prevent-overengineering.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md) | Whether this should exist at all, and at what size |\n41: | Touch a file outside the ask · fix something noticed in passing · change something else because it would make the fix easier · rename, reformat, or delete beyond the named area · deviate from an approved plan · decide what to do with the rest when part of the work is blocked · notice a defect, smell or stale comment outside the files in scope | [`scope-discipline.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md) | What is yours to change, and how to raise what isn't |\n42: | Say \"done\", \"works\", \"fixed\", \"passing\", \"no regressions\" · report a result · quote a number · act on a tool's or sub-agent's success report · close out a turn · report a file path or command output · write a completion summary or tick something off as done | [`evidence-and-proof.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md) | What counts as proof, how a green run lies, what an honest close-out contains |\n43: | Decide whether to hand work to another runtime at all · hand it to a CLI executor, sub-agent, fan-out lineage, or deep loop · compose the prompt one will act on · accept or quote what one returned · answer a judgment question from your own reading alone | [`delegation-and-orchestration.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/delegation-and-orchestration.md) | The orchestrating posture, what a brief must carry, why one model is one opinion |\n44: | Delete, overwrite, migrate, deploy, publish, send, install · force-push or rewrite history · change a shared contract · touch auth, data, or config · make any call that leaves this machine · truncate · touch branches, tags, or reflogs | [`blast-radius.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/blast-radius.md) | Reversibility, the rollback sentence, when to stop for a yes |\n45: | Diagnose a failure · make a red check green · attempt the same fix twice · add a special case, retry, sleep, or broadened catch · call a failure a flake, infra, or pre-existing | [`root-cause-and-debugging.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/root-cause-and-debugging.md) | Fixing the producer instead of the symptom, and when to level up to the seam |\n46: | Answer without certainty · contradict the operator · fill a gap with a plausible guess · hit a contradiction between two things that must both be true · name a path, flag, function, version, or number you have not verified · not know while a plausible answer is available · see sources disagree, or the code contradict the spec, the docs or the operator · the operator asserts something you believe is wrong · change a checked answer after pushback or doubt, or drop an approach before it concludes · cite a rule, restriction, policy, limit or permission, or decline a request | [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/uncertainty-and-honesty.md) | Confidence bands, UNKNOWN, contradiction halts |\n47: | Write any substantive reply, an answer, an explanation, a close-out or a status · draft or edit any sentence a reader will read · present a recommendation, a fork, or a trade-off · start a multi-step stretch of work the reader will not see inside · report what a long autonomous run found · the reader says they did not follow or asks for a plainer version | [`communication.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md) | How a reply reads: answer first, one recommended path, length, filler, plain sentences, punctuation, and no table in a reply |\n48: | End a turn · report work done, blocked, or partly done · end a turn while something you started is still running · ask the operator anything · end a turn on a fork or two acceptable paths · continue past a decision nobody made | [`communication-handoff.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md) | What is the operator's to do next, what is still running and what resumes it, and when a question becomes a structured choice |\n49: | Wire, rewire or remove a mode in a parent-hub skill · edit a hub's registry, router, root `ROUTER.md`, `graph-metadata.json` or `SKILL.md` mode table, or a mode packet's own `ROUTER.md` · report that a mode is registered, routed, reachable or integrated · quote a per-hub gate result · create a new skill under a hub, or refactor one that already lives there | [`skill-hub-routing.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/skill-hub-routing.md) | The two routing stages, what \"integrated\" requires, and checking the hub you actually changed |\n50: \n51: ---\n52: \n53: ## 3. INDEX\n54: \n55: | Rule | Summary |\n56: |------|---------|\n57: | [Overengineering](.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md) | Build the smallest thing that solves the stated problem; take a costlier move only by naming what fails at the cheaper one. |\n58: | [Scope discipline](.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md) | The requested scope is the deliverable. Adjacent problems get named, not fixed. |\n59: | [Evidence and proof](.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md) | A claim is only as strong as the observation behind it, and a receipt confirms only what could have contradicted it. Distinguish observed from derived from inferred. |\n60: | [Delegation and orchestration](.skilled/repo-rules/delegation-and-orchestration.md) | Delegating makes you the orchestrator; brief with evidence, and no single model's verdict closes a question. |\n61: | [Blast radius](.skilled/repo-rules/blast-radius.md) | Size effort to what the change can break; no irreversible step without a named rollback and a yes. |\n62: | [Root cause](.skilled/repo-rules/root-cause-and-debugging.md) | Fix the producer, not the symptom; every fix names the mechanism that caused the failure. |\n63: | [Uncertainty and honesty](.skilled/repo-rules/uncertainty-and-honesty.md) | Never fabricate; mark the confidence you actually have and halt on a contradiction. |\n64: | [Communication](.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md) | Write so the reader can act after one pass: no table in a reply, nothing that does not carry information. |\n65: | [Communication handoff](.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md) | End every turn by naming what is now the operator's to do, in the form that lets them do it. |\n66: | [Hub routing](.skilled/repo-rules/skill-hub-routing.md) | A hub routes in two stages, so a mode registered on one surface is not reachable, and a gate run without its hub argument reports on a hub you did not touch. |\n67: \n68: Each file expands `AGENTS.md` and is bounded by it: where a rule file appears to\n69: permit something `AGENTS.md` restricts, `AGENTS.md` wins and the rule file is wrong.\n70: \n71: ---\n72: \n73: ## 4. SCOPE OF THIS DOCUMENT\n74: \n75: **In:** how to think and act, restraint, scope, evidence, risk, diagnosis, honesty,\n76: the posture to hold when work is handed to another runtime, how the resulting reply\n77: reads, what you may claim about wiring you have changed, and how a turn hands control\n78: back to the operator. The delegation posture was the first widening and Delivery the\n79: second, added when `AGENTS.md` §8 moved down. Delivery is one of two rules here whose\n80: trigger is a whole turn rather than a specific action, the other being the handback rule,\n81: which fires at every turn's end, and `AGENTS.md` §4, under Reply Rules and Mandates, keeps\n82: the two clauses that must bind even when nothing loads.\n83: \n84: **Out:** skill routing, workflow selection, spec-folder mechanics, and the *mechanics*\n85: of agent and CLI dispatch: which agent, which command, which model, which flags.\n86: Those belong to `AGENTS.md` §2 and the skills it routes to, and are deliberately\n87: absent here so each has exactly one place to change. The line is between plumbing and\n88: posture: how to dispatch is theirs, how to think while dispatching is ours.\n89: \n90: **The routing carve-out, added deliberately as the third widening.** *Selecting* a\n91: route stays Out. *Verifying wiring you changed, and what you may claim about it*, is\n92: In; it is an evidence obligation whose subject happens to be routing. `skill-hub-routing.md`\n93: is the one rule on this side of that line, and it carries no route-selection guidance:\n94: the mechanics stay in the skills, and the rule points at them. A fourth widening that\n95: admits selection itself would dissolve the boundary; this one does not.\n96: \n97: **The ask-surface carve-out, added as the fourth widening, on an operator decision that\n98: overrode a research refusal.** A four-iteration decision-test run returned *refuse* here:\n99: naming a question tool per runtime reads as the \"which command\" family, and the paragraph\n100: above pre-refuses a fourth widening that admits selection. The operator widened it anyway,\n101: and what follows is the narrowing that keeps the boundary standing. *Choosing between\n102: runtimes, agents, commands, models or flags* stays Out, unchanged. *Naming the surface that\n103: asks the operator a question, in the runtime you are already running in*, is In. That is not\n104: a selection, because nothing is being selected: the runtime is a given, and the rule records\n105: which of its surfaces carries an obligation the rule already imposes. `communication-handoff.md`\n106: is the one rule on this side of that line, its §5 is the only place a tool name appears, and\n107: it names only what this repository confirms. A fifth widening that let a rule pick between\n108: runtimes would be the dissolution this one avoids."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/REPO RULES.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794543680530,"end":1794543681225}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794543681231,"sessionID":"ses_e2cdd3a02ffe7tBn2LFIz7jxoD","part":{"partID":"prt_1d3237d6f001HooJI9Eoh1iQkG","sessionID":"ses_e2cdd3a02ffe7tBn2LFIz7jxoD","messageID":"msg_1d32368fe001BUxSiUjJmca3rM","type":"tool","id":"call_106f98ed1b0b4709bbc3ed01","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-doc/ROUTER.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-doc/ROUTER.md, lines 1-522\n1: ---\n2: title: sk-doc Surface Router — per-intent leaf sets\n3: description: First-class surface router document at the sk-doc hub root (ROUTER.md). hub-router.json selects the workflow mode; this doc maps a request's documentation/authoring intent to the exact packet-local leaf resources that mode should load, emitting canonical (workflowMode, leafResourceId) pairs. Relocated verbatim from shared/references/smart-routing.md (v1.0.0.0) as the ROUTER.md consolidation pattern.\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"sk-doc surface router\"\n6:   - \"doc authoring resource map\"\n7:   - \"packet leaf resource routing\"\n8:   - \"surface router pattern\"\n9: importance_tier: important\n10: contextType: implementation\n11: version: 2.4.6.0\n12: router_state: active\n13: skill_pointer: SKILL.md\n14: ---\n15: \n16: # sk-doc Surface Router — per-intent leaf sets\n17: \n18: The machine router for [`SKILL.md`](./SKILL.md). `SKILL.md` keeps the short routing prose and the link that points here; this file holds the declaration a reader parses to decide what loads.\n19: \n20: ---\n21: \n22: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n23: \n24: This is sk-doc's second-layer (surface) router, first-class at the hub root as\n25: `ROUTER.md`. The hub selects a workflow mode in [`hub-router.json`](hub-router.json)\n26: (sk-create-skill, sk-create-skill-parent, sk-create-readme, sk-create-agent, sk-create-command,\n27: sk-create-feature-catalog, sk-create-manual-testing-playbook, sk-create-benchmark,\n28: sk-create-changelog, sk-create-diff, sk-create-repo-rule, sk-create-frontmatter,\n29: sk-create-goal, sk-create-with-human-voice, or sk-create-quality-control);\n30: this doc maps a request's documentation/authoring intent to the exact packet-local\n31: leaf resources that mode should load. Every path is either packet-qualified\n32: (`<packet>/references|assets/…`) or an authored shared-alias disk path (`shared/…`),\n33: and both convert to the canonical `(workflowMode, leafResourceId)` pair at the\n34: contract boundary (`sk-doc/sk-create-skill/scripts/lib/leaf-resource-contract.cjs`).\n35: `FULL_INVENTORY` is the single explicit full-toolkit intent — no other intent\n36: enumerates the whole hub.\n37: \n38: Routing is two stages: the hub picks the WORKFLOW mode (mode telemetry), this\n39: router picks the LEAVES within it. The two layers stay separate — the hub never\n40: emits leaf paths, and this router never re-decides the mode.\n41: \n42: ---\n43: \n44: ## 2. INTENT MODEL\n45: \n46: - **doc-quality leaves** — the validation rules, core standards, evergreen\n47:   packet-id rule, and the quality-control workflow a request to validate, audit,\n48:   or score documentation against sk-doc standards loads. Fired by \"documentation\n49:   quality / validate documentation / validate a document / score this document /\n50:   DQI / document audit / review the docs / document for clarity / audit the docs /\n51:   pass review\"\n52:   requests. The scoring phrases mirror `hub-router.json`, so a request that\n53:   reaches the quality mode at stage one also reaches its leaves at stage two.\n54: - **optimization leaves** — the optimization reference and the llms.txt templates\n55:   a request to compress, trim, or token-optimize a doc, or emit a machine-readable\n56:   index loads. Fired by \"optimize / token efficiency / llms.txt / reduce tokens /\n57:   slim down\" requests.\n58: - **skill-creation leaves** — the skill creation workflow and the skill.md /\n59:   readme / reference templates a request to scaffold or author a new skill loads.\n60:   Fired by \"create a skill / skill.md scaffold / resource_map wiring / package a\n61:   capability\" requests.\n62: - **parent-hub leaves.** The nested-packet contract, the hub-router schema and\n63:   the four parent-hub templates a request to build or rewire a parent hub loads.\n64:   Fired by \"parent hub / parent skill / mode packet / nested workflow packet /\n65:   mode-registry.json / hub-router.json\" requests, the file names spelled in full\n66:   so a bare \"hub router\" in an unrelated sentence does not fire it. This is the leaf set for the\n67:   `sk-create-skill-parent` mode, which shares the `sk-create-skill` packet but\n68:   not its leaves: a plain skill request must not pull the hub templates.\n69: - **render-ledger leaves.** The validation reference that explains the render\n70:   ledger's debt, exempt and pinned records and the doc rewrite guard, and the\n71:   maintenance workflow that runs the guard before a rewrite is committed. Fired\n72:   by \"doc rewrite guard / render ledger / render-known-debt.json / exempt from\n73:   the render checks / kept every claim\" requests. It belongs to the\n74:   `sk-create-skill` mode, which owns the ledger.\n75: - **agent-creation leaves.** The agent README and agent template a request to\n76:   scaffold an OpenCode agent loads. Fired by \"create an agent / new agent /\n77:   agent frontmatter / permission object / @analyze agent\" requests.\n78: - **command-creation leaves.** The command README and command template a\n79:   request to scaffold a slash command loads. Fired by \"create a command / slash\n80:   command / argument-hint / allowed-tools / thin router\" requests.\n81: - **agent-command leaves.** Both packets' READMEs and templates a request to\n82:   author an agent together with its paired `/create` command loads. Fired only\n83:   by the two paired phrases, \"agent and paired\" and \"paired /create\", which no\n84:   single-artifact request carries. The paired intent stays separate rather than\n85:   being expressed as the union of the two above, because the router keeps only\n86:   intents within one point of the top score: a paired request that also names\n87:   the agent would otherwise drop the command half.\n88: - **diagram and chart leaves belong to the `sk-design` hub.** ASCII flowchart,\n89:   decision-tree and standalone HTML chart authoring is `sk-design-diagram` and\n90:   `sk-design-chart` work, and this router declares no intent for it: `/design:diagram`\n91:   and `/design:chart` are the entry points, so \"flowchart\", \"ascii\", \"process diagram\",\n92:   \"create a chart\" and \"plot the data\" reach no key in the two maps below.\n93: - **human-voice leaves** — the scope gate, the scoring method and the Human\n94:   Voice Rules standard a request to apply or score that standard loads. Fired\n95:   by \"hvr / human voice rules / apply human voice / rewrite in human voice /\n96:   make this sound human / sounds ai-generated / remove ai tells\" requests. The\n97:   scope gate is the always-loaded leaf, because deciding what a voice edit may\n98:   touch comes before deciding what to change.\n99: - **playbook leaves** — the manual-testing-playbook README a request to author a\n100:   manual testing playbook loads. Fired by \"playbook system / manual testing\n101:   playbook / testing playbook / playbook scenario / manual test\n102:   scenario\" requests.\n103: - **playbook-conversation leaves** — the conversation-scenario reference and the\n104:   conversation snippet template a request to write a multi-turn json conversation script for\n105:   a playbook scenario loads. Fired by \"playbook conversation / multi-turn\n106:   conversation script / json conversation / conversation runner\" requests. This\n107:   intent scores double. \"manual testing playbook\" also matches \"testing\n108:   playbook\", so that one phrase scores the playbook intent twice, and at equal\n109:   weight a request naming both a playbook and a conversation would load only the\n110:   playbook README. At double weight the two tie and both leaf sets load.\n111: - **feature-catalog leaves** — the feature-catalog README a request to inventory a\n112:   project's features / capabilities loads. Fired by \"feature catalog / feature\n113:   inventory / capabilities\" requests.\n114: - **readme-creation leaves** — the readme README and readme template a request to\n115:   author a project README / front-page overview loads. Fired by \"create a readme /\n116:   readme for / project overview / landing doc\" requests.\n117: - **changelog leaves** — the changelog template a request to author release notes\n118:   / version notes loads. Fired by \"changelog / release notes / what shipped /\n119:   since the last version\" requests.\n120: - **benchmark leaves** — the benchmark shared README, worked example, and report\n121:   template a request to author a behavior benchmark\n122:   loads. Fired by \"create a benchmark / benchmark suite / behavior benchmark\"\n123:   requests.\n124: - **diff leaves** — the diff README, workflow, and worked example a request to\n125:   produce a before/after document diff loads. Fired by \"document diff / before\n126:   and after diff / visual document diff\" requests.\n127: - **repo-rule leaves** — the four decision tests, the rule anatomy contract, the\n128:   creation standards, the wiring contract, the rule and router templates, and the\n129:   packet reference map a\n130:   request to add, change, or remove a repo-local rule loads. Fired by \"repo rule /\n131:   project rule / REPO RULES.md / trigger table / retire a repo rule\" requests. Most such\n132:   requests end in a refusal, so `decision-tests.md` is the always-loaded leaf.\n133: - **frontmatter leaves** — the field reference and the versioning standard a\n134:   request about a frontmatter block itself loads. Fired by \"yaml frontmatter /\n135:   frontmatter block / frontmatter field / description budget / 4-part version /\n136:   frontmatter versioning\" requests. The phrases are qualified rather than bare,\n137:   because `agent frontmatter` belongs to agent-creation and a bare \"frontmatter\"\n138:   would take that route away from it. The field reference is the always-loaded\n139:   leaf, because resolving the document class comes before any field rule.\n140: - **goal-authoring leaves** — the goal authoring standards, the goal exemplars,\n141:   the parent/nested goal workflows and the budget/handoff reference a request to\n142:   author or revise a packet's `goal.md` loads. Fired by \"packet goal / goal.md /\n143:   goal document / author goal.md / phase parent goal / goal criteria /\n144:   completion checks\" requests. The phrases are qualified rather than bare,\n145:   because a bare \"goal\" and the session verbs belong to session-goal management\n146:   (set, bind, update, resend), which the goal hooks and host commands own. The\n147:   authoring standards are the always-loaded leaf, because what a goal must state\n148:   comes before how one is phrased.\n149: - **full-inventory leaves** — the entire sk-doc toolkit. `FULL_INVENTORY` is the\n150:   single explicit full-toolkit intent; no other intent enumerates the whole hub.\n151:   Fired only by an explicit \"show the full sk-doc toolkit / everything sk-doc\n152:   offers\" request.\n153: \n154: A single dominant documentation axis routes to one mode's leaf set; two clearly\n155: separate axes route to both.\n156: \n157: ---\n158: \n159: ## 3. MACHINE-READABLE ROUTER (replay / benchmark source)\n160: \n161: The single machine-readable projection of the intent model above. The prose is the\n162: human-facing contract; this block is the byte-for-byte source the deterministic\n163: router-replay parses. Keep them in sync: when a map row changes above, update the\n164: matching `RESOURCE_MAP` entry here. Every `RESOURCE_MAP` path resolves on disk and\n165: dual-reads to a canonical typed pair through `leaf-manifest.json` or the hub's\n166: `leaf-aliases.json`.\n167: \n168: ```python\n169: INTENT_SIGNALS = {\n170:     \"DOC_QUALITY\": {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"documentation quality\", \"doc quality\", \"validate documentation\", \"validate a document\", \"validate this document\", \"validate markdown\", \"validation rules\", \"score this document\", \"score this doc\", \"dqi\", \"document audit\", \"fail sk-doc standards\", \"review the docs\", \"review the documentation\", \"document for clarity\", \"check the docs\", \"check the documentation\", \"audit the docs\", \"documentation review bar\", \"quality bar\", \"flag\", \"meet our standards\", \"audit documentation quality\", \"ai-friendly documentation\", \"extract structure\", \"validate\", \"score\", \"harden\", \"audit\", \"/doc:quality\"]},\n171:     \"OPTIMIZATION\": {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"optimize\", \"token efficiency\", \"llms.txt\", \"llmstxt\", \"reduce tokens\", \"fewer tokens\", \"trim\", \"compress the doc\", \"slim down\", \"model's budget\", \"machine-readable index\", \"optimize this doc\"]},\n172:     \"SKILL_CREATION\": {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"sk-skill\", \"create a new sk\", \"create sk-\", \"skill.md scaffold\", \"skill.md and starter\", \"resource_map wiring\", \"new skill\", \"create a skill\", \"build a skill\", \"author a skill\", \"scaffold a skill\", \"reusable capability\", \"reusable helper\", \"starter reference docs\", \"new capability\", \"capability module\", \"package a capability\", \"create skill\", \"skill scaffold\", \"opencode skill\", \"package skill\", \"which json files does a skill need\", \"skill root metadata contract\", \"leaf-manifest config aliases required\", \"/create:skill\"]},\n173:     \"PARENT_HUB\": {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"parent hub\", \"parent skill\", \"mode packet\", \"mode packets\", \"nested packet\", \"nested workflow packet\", \"mode-registry.json\", \"hub-router.json\", \"hub versus standalone skill class\", \"skill hub\", \"/create:skill-parent\"]},\n174:     \"RENDER_LEDGER\": {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"doc rewrite guard\", \"doc-rewrite-guard.py\", \"rewrite guard\", \"render ledger\", \"render-known-debt.json\", \"exempt from the render checks\", \"render exempt map\", \"kept every claim\", \"kept every original claim\"]},\n175:     \"AGENT_CREATION\": {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"create an agent\", \"create agent\", \"new agent\", \"author an agent\", \"scaffold an agent\", \"agent file\", \"agent persona\", \"agent frontmatter\", \"agent template\", \"permission object\", \"authority boundary\", \"@analyze agent\", \"opencode agent\", \"claude code agent\", \"/create:agent\"]},\n176:     \"COMMAND_CREATION\": {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"create a command\", \"create command\", \"slash command\", \"new slash command\", \"author a command\", \"scaffold a command\", \"argument-hint\", \"allowed-tools\", \"command template\", \"router presentation split\", \"thin router\", \"presentation contract\", \"opencode command\", \"$arguments\", \"/create:command\"]},\n177:     \"AGENT_COMMAND\": {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"agent and paired\", \"paired /create\"]},\n178:     \"HVR\": {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"hvr\", \"human voice rules\", \"apply human voice\", \"rewrite in human voice\", \"make this sound human\", \"sounds ai-generated\", \"reads like ai wrote it\", \"reads like a machine wrote it\", \"remove ai tells\", \"ai writing tells\", \"voice pass\", \"de-ai the writing\", \"banned word check\", \"human voice\"]},\n179:     \"PLAYBOOK\": {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"playbook system\", \"manual testing playbook\", \"testing playbook\", \"deterministic scenario\", \"evidence collection\", \"operator validation\", \"multi-agent execution\", \"release readiness\", \"playbook scenario\", \"manual test scenario\", \"/create:manual-testing-playbook\"]},\n180:     \"PLAYBOOK_CONVERSATION\": {\"weight\": 8, \"keywords\": [\"playbook conversation\", \"multi-turn conversation script\", \"json conversation\", \"conversation runner\", \"run-conversation-scenario\"]},\n181:     \"FEATURE_CATALOG\": {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"feature catalog\", \"feature inventory\", \"catalog of features\", \"features overview\", \"capabilities\", \"catalog package\", \"per-feature files\", \"source anchors\", \"root catalog\", \"capability inventory\", \"/create:feature-catalog\"]},\n182:     \"README_CREATION\": {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"create a readme\", \"readme for\", \"a readme\", \"front-page overview\", \"project overview\", \"getting started doc\", \"intro doc\", \"overview doc\", \"landing doc\", \"create readme\", \"readme\", \"folder readme\", \"write readme\", \"readme.md\", \"code folder readme\", \"/create:readme\"]},\n183:     \"CHANGELOG\": {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"changelog\", \"release notes\", \"version notes\", \"release summary\", \"what shipped\", \"since the last version\", \"changelog entry\", \"nested changelog\", \"packet changelog\", \"packet-local changelog\", \"create changelog\", \"global changelog\", \"vx.y.z.b\", \"/create:changelog\"]},\n184:     \"BENCHMARK\": {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"create a benchmark\", \"author a benchmark\", \"benchmark suite\", \"benchmark authoring\", \"behavior benchmark\", \"benchmark-report.md\", \"source.md\", \"mcp-server/benchmarks\", \"behavior-benchmark.md\", \"scenario contract\", \"claude-baseline\", \"benchmark/readme.md\", \"run-label folder\", \"benchmark package\", \"benchmark fixture\", \"benchmark profile\", \"reviewer-prompt fixture\", \"agent quality dimensions\", \"new benchmark\", \"promote benchmark folder\", \"create benchmark\", \"behavior_benchmark\"]},\n185:     \"DIFF\": {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"document diff\", \"doc diff\", \"diff document\", \"before and after diff\", \"before/after diff\", \"visual document diff\", \"create diff report\", \"document before after review\", \"before after document diff\", \"document change report\", \"before and after document\", \"document before/after review\", \"before/after document diff\", \"review document edits\", \"docx diff\", \"pdf diff\"]},\n186:     \"REPO_RULE\": {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"repo rule\", \"repo-rules\", \"repo rules\", \"project rule\", \"repo rule file\", \"REPO RULES.md\", \"trigger table\", \"rule router\", \"retire a rule\", \"retire a repo rule\", \"revise a rule\", \"revise a repo rule\", \"add a repo rule\", \"always-loaded rule\", \"rule that binds\", \"create repo rule\", \"new repo rule\", \"repo-local rule\", \"we should always\", \"stop doing\", \"add a rule\", \"/create:repo-rule\"]},\n187:     \"FRONTMATTER\": {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"yaml frontmatter\", \"frontmatter block\", \"frontmatter template\", \"frontmatter field\", \"frontmatter fields\", \"trigger_phrases\", \"trigger phrases\", \"importance_tier\", \"contextType\", \"description budget\", \"4-part version\", \"X.Y.Z.W\", \"frontmatter versioning\", \"frontmatter version\", \"version field\", \"frontmatter validation\", \"missing frontmatter\", \"frontmatter contract\", \"yaml header\", \"goes at the top of the file\", \"version number at the top\", \"importance tier\", \"versioning pass\", \"stopped showing up in suggestions\", \"validator says my file is missing\", \"description too long\", \"edit count\", \"field the validator wants\"]},\n188:     \"GOAL_AUTHORING\": {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"create packet goal\", \"packet goal\", \"goal.md\", \"author goal.md\", \"revise packet goal\", \"phase parent goal\", \"nested phase child goal\", \"nested goal\", \"add a goal file\", \"write a goal file\", \"draft a goal file\", \"goal file\", \"goal document\", \"goal criteria\", \"completion checks\", \"measurable criteria\", \"durable objective\", \"phase objective\", \"parent objective\", \"objective for a packet\", \"phase-child goal\", \"child-phase goal\", \"binding table\", \"goal chat slice\", \"/create:goal\"]},\n189:     \"FULL_INVENTORY\": {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"full sk-doc toolkit\", \"all templates\", \"entire toolkit\", \"everything sk-doc offers\"]},\n190: }\n191: \n192: RESOURCE_MAP = {\n193:     \"DOC_QUALITY\": [\n194:         \"shared/references/validation.md\",\n195:         \"sk-create-quality-control/references/workflows.md\",\n196:         \"shared/references/core-standards.md\",\n197:         \"shared/references/evergreen-packet-id-rule.md\"\n198:     ],\n199:     \"OPTIMIZATION\": [\n200:         \"sk-create-quality-control/references/optimization.md\",\n201:         \"shared/assets/llmstxt-templates.md\"\n202:     ],\n203:     \"SKILL_CREATION\": [\n204:         \"sk-create-skill/references/skill/creation-workflow.md\",\n205:         \"sk-create-skill/assets/skill/skill-md-template.md\",\n206:         \"sk-create-skill/assets/skill/skill-readme-template.md\",\n207:         \"sk-create-skill/assets/skill/skill-reference-template.md\"\n208:     ],\n209:     \"RENDER_LEDGER\": [\n210:         \"sk-create-skill/references/shared/validation-and-packaging.md\",\n211:         \"sk-create-skill/references/skill/examples-and-maintenance.md\"\n212:     ],\n213:     \"PARENT_HUB\": [\n214:         \"sk-create-skill/references/parent-skill/parent-skills-nested-packets.md\",\n215:         \"sk-create-skill/references/parent-skill/parent-hub-router-schema.md\",\n216:         \"sk-create-skill/assets/parent-skill/parent-skill-hub-template.md\",\n217:         \"sk-create-skill/assets/parent-skill/parent-skill-registry-template.json\",\n218:         \"sk-create-skill/assets/parent-skill/parent-skill-hub-router-template.json\",\n219:         \"sk-create-skill/assets/parent-skill/parent-skill-root-router-template.md\"\n220:     ],\n221:     \"AGENT_CREATION\": [\n222:         \"sk-create-agent/references/README.md\",\n223:         \"sk-create-agent/assets/agent-template.md\"\n224:     ],\n225:     \"COMMAND_CREATION\": [\n226:         \"sk-create-command/references/README.md\",\n227:         \"sk-create-command/assets/command-template.md\"\n228:     ],\n229:     \"AGENT_COMMAND\": [\n230:         \"sk-create-agent/references/README.md\",\n231:         \"sk-create-agent/assets/agent-template.md\",\n232:         \"sk-create-command/references/README.md\",\n233:         \"sk-create-command/assets/command-template.md\"\n234:     ],\n235:     \"HVR\": [\n236:         \"sk-create-with-human-voice/references/scope-and-exemptions.md\",\n237:         \"sk-create-with-human-voice/references/scoring-and-verification.md\",\n238:         \"sk-create-with-human-voice/assets/voice-report-template.md\",\n239:         \"sk-create-with-human-voice/references/hvr-rules.md\"\n240:     ],\n241:     \"PLAYBOOK\": [\n242:         \"sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/references/README.md\"\n243:     ],\n244:     \"PLAYBOOK_CONVERSATION\": [\n245:         \"sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/references/conversation-scenarios.md\",\n246:         \"sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/assets/manual-testing-playbook-conversation-snippet-template.md\"\n247:     ],\n248:     \"FEATURE_CATALOG\": [\n249:         \"sk-create-feature-catalog/references/README.md\"\n250:     ],\n251:     \"README_CREATION\": [\n252:         \"sk-create-readme/references/README.md\",\n253:         \"sk-create-readme/assets/readme-template.md\"\n254:     ],\n255:     \"CHANGELOG\": [\n256:         \"sk-create-changelog/assets/changelog-template.md\"\n257:     ],\n258:     \"BENCHMARK\": [\n259:         \"sk-create-benchmark/references/shared/README.md\",\n260:         \"sk-create-benchmark/references/shared/worked-example.md\",\n261:         \"sk-create-benchmark/assets/shared/benchmark-report-template.md\"\n262:     ],\n263:     \"DIFF\": [\n264:         \"sk-create-diff/references/README.md\",\n265:         \"sk-create-diff/references/workflow.md\",\n266:         \"sk-create-diff/references/worked-example.md\"\n267:     ],\n268:     \"REPO_RULE\": [\n269:         \"sk-create-repo-rule/references/decision-tests.md\",\n270:         \"sk-create-repo-rule/references/rule-anatomy.md\",\n271:         \"sk-create-repo-rule/references/creation-standards.md\",\n272:         \"sk-create-repo-rule/references/agents-md-integration.md\",\n273:         \"sk-create-repo-rule/assets/repo-rule-template.md\",\n274:         \"sk-create-repo-rule/assets/repo-rules-router-template.md\",\n275:         \"sk-create-repo-rule/references/README.md\"\n276:     ],\n277:     \"FRONTMATTER\": [\n278:         \"sk-create-frontmatter/assets/frontmatter-templates.md\",\n279:         \"sk-create-frontmatter/references/frontmatter-versioning.md\",\n280:         \"sk-create-frontmatter/references/README.md\"\n281:     ],\n282:     \"GOAL_AUTHORING\": [\n283:         \"sk-create-goal/references/authoring-standards.md\",\n284:         \"sk-create-goal/assets/goal-exemplars.md\",\n285:         \"sk-create-goal/references/parent-and-nested-goals.md\",\n286:         \"sk-create-goal/references/budget-and-handoff.md\",\n287:         \"sk-create-goal/assets/goal-top-level-template.md\",\n288:         \"sk-create-goal/assets/goal-phase-parent-template.md\",\n289:         \"sk-create-goal/assets/goal-phase-child-template.md\"\n290:     ],\n291:     \"FULL_INVENTORY\": [\n292:         \"sk-create-agent/assets/agent-template.md\",\n293:         \"sk-create-agent/references/README.md\",\n294:         \"sk-create-agent/references/agent-vs-skill-vs-command.md\",\n295:         \"sk-create-agent/references/common-pitfalls.md\",\n296:         \"sk-create-agent/references/permission-design.md\",\n297:         \"sk-create-frontmatter/assets/fixtures/README.md\",\n298:         \"sk-create-frontmatter/assets/fixtures/over-budget-description.md\",\n299:         \"sk-create-frontmatter/assets/fixtures/under-budget-trim-lost-tokens.md\",\n300:         \"sk-create-frontmatter/assets/frontmatter-templates.md\",\n301:         \"sk-create-frontmatter/references/README.md\",\n302:         \"sk-create-frontmatter/references/frontmatter-versioning.md\",\n303:         \"sk-create-goal/assets/goal-exemplars.md\",\n304:         \"sk-create-goal/assets/goal-phase-child-template.md\",\n305:         \"sk-create-goal/assets/goal-phase-parent-template.md\",\n306:         \"sk-create-goal/assets/goal-top-level-template.md\",\n307:         \"sk-create-goal/references/authoring-standards.md\",\n308:         \"sk-create-goal/references/budget-and-handoff.md\",\n309:         \"sk-create-goal/references/parent-and-nested-goals.md\",\n310:         \"sk-create-benchmark/assets/behavior-benchmark/behavior-benchmark-baseline-template.md\",\n311:         \"sk-create-benchmark/assets/behavior-benchmark/behavior-benchmark-index-template.md\",\n312:         \"sk-create-benchmark/assets/behavior-benchmark/behavior-benchmark-scenario-template.md\",\n313:         \"sk-create-benchmark/assets/shared/benchmark-report-template.md\",\n314:         \"sk-create-benchmark/assets/shared/source-template.md\",\n315:         \"sk-create-benchmark/references/behavior-benchmark/behavior-benchmark-guide.md\",\n316:         \"sk-create-benchmark/references/shared/README.md\",\n317:         \"sk-create-benchmark/references/shared/case-studies.md\",\n318:         \"sk-create-benchmark/references/shared/pitfalls.md\",\n319:         \"sk-create-benchmark/references/shared/worked-example.md\",\n320:         \"sk-create-changelog/assets/changelog-template.md\",\n321:         \"sk-create-changelog/references/README.md\",\n322:         \"sk-create-changelog/references/topology-edge-cases.md\",\n323:         \"sk-create-changelog/references/version-bump-rules.md\",\n324:         \"sk-create-changelog/references/worked-examples.md\",\n325:         \"sk-create-command/assets/command-contract.json\",\n326:         \"sk-create-command/assets/command-contract.schema.json\",\n327:         \"sk-create-command/assets/command-presentation-template.md\",\n328:         \"sk-create-command/assets/command-router-template.md\",\n329:         \"sk-create-command/assets/command-template.md\",\n330:         \"sk-create-command/references/README.md\",\n331:         \"sk-create-command/references/argument-hints-and-modes.md\",\n332:         \"sk-create-command/references/common-pitfalls.md\",\n333:         \"sk-create-command/references/router-presentation-split.md\",\n334:         \"sk-create-command/references/worked-example.md\",\n335:         \"sk-create-diff/assets/fixtures/README.md\",\n336:         \"sk-create-diff/assets/fixtures/onboarding-after.md\",\n337:         \"sk-create-diff/assets/fixtures/onboarding-before.md\",\n338:         \"sk-create-diff/references/README.md\",\n339:         \"sk-create-diff/references/accessibility-contract.md\",\n340:         \"sk-create-diff/references/capabilities-and-fidelity.md\",\n341:         \"sk-create-diff/references/cli-reference.md\",\n342:         \"sk-create-diff/references/worked-example.md\",\n343:         \"sk-create-diff/references/workflow.md\",\n344:         \"sk-create-feature-catalog/assets/feature-catalog-snippet-template.md\",\n345:         \"sk-create-feature-catalog/assets/feature-catalog-template.md\",\n346:         \"sk-create-feature-catalog/references/README.md\",\n347:         \"sk-create-feature-catalog/references/common-pitfalls.md\",\n348:         \"sk-create-feature-catalog/references/examples.md\",\n349:         \"sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/assets/manual-testing-playbook-conversation-snippet-template.md\",\n350:         \"sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/assets/manual-testing-playbook-snippet-template.md\",\n351:         \"sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/assets/manual-testing-playbook-template.md\",\n352:         \"sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/references/README.md\",\n353:         \"sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/references/common-pitfalls.md\",\n354:         \"sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/references/conversation-scenarios.md\",\n355:         \"sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/references/examples.md\",\n356:         \"sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/references/prompt-voice.md\",\n357:         \"shared/assets/llmstxt-templates.md\",\n358:         \"shared/references/core-standards.md\",\n359:         \"shared/references/evergreen-packet-id-rule.md\",\n360:         \"shared/references/validation.md\",\n361:         \"sk-create-quality-control/references/README.md\",\n362:         \"sk-create-quality-control/references/optimization.md\",\n363:         \"sk-create-quality-control/references/transformation-patterns.md\",\n364:         \"sk-create-quality-control/references/validation-and-enforcement.md\",\n365:         \"sk-create-quality-control/references/workflow-examples.md\",\n366:         \"sk-create-quality-control/references/workflows.md\",\n367:         \"sk-create-readme/assets/architecture-template.md\",\n368:         \"sk-create-readme/assets/readme-code-template.md\",\n369:         \"sk-create-readme/assets/readme-template.md\",\n370:         \"sk-create-readme/references/README.md\",\n371:         \"sk-create-readme/references/readme/quality-and-checklist.md\",\n372:         \"sk-create-readme/references/readme/types-and-voice.md\",\n373:         \"sk-create-readme/references/readme/writing-patterns.md\",\n374:         \"sk-create-repo-rule/assets/repo-rule-template.md\",\n375:         \"sk-create-repo-rule/assets/repo-rules-router-template.md\",\n376:         \"sk-create-repo-rule/references/README.md\",\n377:         \"sk-create-repo-rule/references/agents-md-integration.md\",\n378:         \"sk-create-repo-rule/references/creation-standards.md\",\n379:         \"sk-create-repo-rule/references/decision-tests.md\",\n380:         \"sk-create-repo-rule/references/rule-anatomy.md\",\n381:         \"sk-create-with-human-voice/assets/voice-report-template.md\",\n382:         \"sk-create-with-human-voice/references/README.md\",\n383:         \"sk-create-with-human-voice/references/hvr-rules.md\",\n384:         \"sk-create-with-human-voice/references/scope-and-exemptions.md\",\n385:         \"sk-create-with-human-voice/references/scoring-and-verification.md\",\n386:         \"sk-create-skill/assets/parent-skill/parent-skill-command-metadata-template.json\",\n387:         \"sk-create-skill/assets/parent-skill/parent-skill-description-template.json\",\n388:         \"sk-create-skill/assets/parent-skill/parent-skill-graph-metadata-template.json\",\n389:         \"sk-create-skill/assets/parent-skill/parent-skill-hub-router-template.json\",\n390:         \"sk-create-skill/assets/parent-skill/parent-skill-hub-template.md\",\n391:         \"sk-create-skill/assets/parent-skill/parent-skill-leaf-aliases-template.json\",\n392:         \"sk-create-skill/assets/parent-skill/parent-skill-readme-template.md\",\n393:         \"sk-create-skill/assets/parent-skill/parent-skill-registry-template.json\",\n394:         \"sk-create-skill/assets/parent-skill/parent-skill-root-router-template.md\",\n395:         \"sk-create-skill/assets/parent-skill/scaffold/hub-skill-scaffold.md\",\n396:         \"sk-create-skill/assets/parent-skill/scaffold/packet-skill-scaffold.md\",\n397:         \"sk-create-skill/assets/skill/skill-asset-template.md\",\n398:         \"sk-create-skill/assets/skill/skill-graph-metadata-template.json\",\n399:         \"sk-create-skill/assets/skill/skill-leaf-manifest-config-template.json\",\n400:         \"sk-create-skill/assets/skill/skill-md-template.md\",\n401:         \"sk-create-skill/assets/skill/skill-procedure-template.md\",\n402:         \"sk-create-skill/assets/skill/skill-readme-template.md\",\n403:         \"sk-create-skill/assets/skill/skill-reference-template.md\",\n404:         \"sk-create-skill/assets/skill/skill-scaffold-template.md\",\n405:         \"sk-create-skill/assets/skill/skill-smart-router.md\",\n406:         \"sk-create-skill/assets/skill/skill-sync-manifest-template.md\",\n407:         \"sk-create-skill/references/README.md\",\n408:         \"sk-create-skill/references/parent-skill/compiled-routing-architecture.md\",\n409:         \"sk-create-skill/references/parent-skill/compiled-routing-lockstep-surfaces.json\",\n410:         \"sk-create-skill/references/parent-skill/parent-hub-router-schema.md\",\n411:         \"sk-create-skill/references/parent-skill/parent-skills-nested-packets.md\",\n412:         \"sk-create-skill/references/shared/advisor-index-handoff.md\",\n413:         \"sk-create-skill/references/shared/common-pitfalls.md\",\n414:         \"sk-create-skill/references/shared/overview.md\",\n415:         \"sk-create-skill/references/shared/skill-root-metadata-contract.md\",\n416:         \"sk-create-skill/references/shared/validation-and-packaging.md\",\n417:         \"sk-create-skill/references/skill/creation-workflow.md\",\n418:         \"sk-create-skill/references/skill/examples-and-maintenance.md\",\n419:         \"sk-create-skill/references/skill/upgrading-a-skill-to-v4.md\"\n420:     ],\n421: }\n422: ```\n423: \n424: ---\n425: \n426: ## 4. HOW TO READ THIS\n427: \n428: - One dominant intent routes to one mode's leaf set.\n429: - Two near-tied intents (within the ambiguity delta) route to both leaf sets; the\n430:   union is deduped by canonical pair.\n431: - No keyword match is UNKNOWN_FALLBACK: confirm the target artifact and intent\n432:   before loading anything.\n433: - `FULL_INVENTORY` fires only on an explicit \"show the whole toolkit\" request.\n434: - A mode whose `mode-registry.json` row names a `command` has two whole entries: read that command's document in full and follow it under the policy the advisor names, or follow this mode's packet. Pick one and keep to it. A mode whose row has `command: null` has only its packet.\n435: \n436: ---\n437: \n438: ## 5. MACHINE ROUTER\n439: \n440: The hub's detailed routing prose, moved here from `SKILL.md` so the skill body keeps only a short routing summary and a pointer to this file. It sits last, after the stage-two map above, and it declares no map of its own: the `INTENT_SIGNALS` and `RESOURCE_MAP` block in section 3 stays the only replay source.\n441: \n442: > **Compiled routing (default-on, flag-gated, additive).** Resolve the mode via the compiled router contract first:\n443: > ```bash\n444: > node .skilled/bin/compiled-route.cjs --hub sk-doc --prompt \"<task>\"\n445: > ```\n446: > Follow the returned decision — `route` (use its `targets`), `clarify`/`defer` (disambiguate), `reject` (refuse). On a `{\"servingAuthority\":\"legacy\"}` sentinel or any error, use the routing below. The front door self-gates on serving-authority. Compiled routing is now the default for `sk-doc`; set `SPECKIT_COMPILED_ROUTING=0` to force legacy routing fleet-wide — the explicit kill-switch.\n447: \n448: ### Surface Router — per-intent leaf sets\n449: \n450: Stage 2 of routing lives in section 3 of this file.\n451: It defines three things:\n452: - The per-intent leaf model: document quality, optimization, skill creation, parent-hub creation, agent creation, command creation, the paired agent-plus-command lane, the human-voice pass, playbooks, playbook conversations, feature catalogs, READMEs, changelogs, benchmarks, diffs, frontmatter, packet goals, repo rules, and the explicit full-toolkit intent.\n453: - The machine-readable `INTENT_SIGNALS` / `RESOURCE_MAP` block that the deterministic router-replay and benchmarks parse.\n454: - The how-to-read rules: a dominant intent gives one leaf set, near-tied intents give a deduped union, and no keyword match gives the hub UNKNOWN fallback, never a silent default.\n455: \n456: Every `RESOURCE_MAP` path is packet-qualified or an authored shared-alias disk path, and each converts to the canonical `(workflowMode, leafResourceId)` pair at the one contract boundary.\n457: \n458: The reason this file stays a separate document from `SKILL.md` and from `hub-router.json` is in [the parent-skill reference](sk-create-skill/references/parent-skill/parent-skills-nested-packets.md#why-the-leaf-router-stays-in-routermd).\n459: \n460: ### The discriminator\n461: - **`workflowMode`** — the public packet key (e.g. `sk-create-skill`, `sk-create-quality-control`). `sk-create-skill-parent` is a second mode over the same `sk-create-skill` packet.\n462: - **`packetKind`** — `workflow` for every sk-doc packet (there is no surface axis; the sk-create-quality-control pipeline is universal doctrine in `shared/`, not orthogonal stack-evidence).\n463: - **`backendKind`** — `template-scaffold` for the create-* generators, `sk-create-quality-control` for the sk-create-quality-control mode.\n464: \n465: ### Routing rule\n466: ```\n467: SKILL_ROOT = path containing this ROUTER.md and its SKILL.md\n468: REGISTRY = SKILL_ROOT / \"mode-registry.json\"\n469: HUB_ROUTER = SKILL_ROOT / \"hub-router.json\"\n470: \n471: UNKNOWN_FALLBACK_CHECKLIST = [\n472:     \"Confirm whether this is sk-create-skill, sk-create-skill-parent, sk-create-readme, sk-create-agent, sk-create-command, sk-create-feature-catalog, sk-create-manual-testing-playbook, sk-create-benchmark, sk-create-changelog, sk-create-diff, sk-create-repo-rule, sk-create-frontmatter, sk-create-goal, sk-create-with-human-voice, or sk-create-quality-control work\",\n473:     \"Confirm the target document or component being authored, scaffolded, or reviewed\",\n474:     \"Provide the available inputs: existing doc, source material, or scaffold parameters\",\n475:     \"Confirm the validation expectations before completion (DQI/HVR scoring, validate.sh, or packet-specific checks)\",\n476: ]\n477: \n478: def _guard_in_skill(relative_path):\n479:   resolved = (SKILL_ROOT / relative_path).resolve()\n480:   resolved.relative_to(SKILL_ROOT)\n481:   if resolved.suffix.lower() not in {\".md\", \".json\"}:\n482:     raise ValueError(\"only skill-local markdown/json router resources are routable\")\n483:   return resolved.relative_to(SKILL_ROOT).as_posix()\n484: \n485: def load_if_available(relative_path, seen):\n486:   guarded = _guard_in_skill(relative_path)\n487:   if guarded not in seen and (SKILL_ROOT / guarded).exists():\n488:     load(guarded)\n489:     seen.add(guarded)\n490:     return True\n491:   return False\n492: \n493: seen = set()\n494: if not REGISTRY.exists() or not HUB_ROUTER.exists():\n495:   return defer(\"router metadata missing; inspect sk-doc/mode-registry.json and sk-doc/hub-router.json\")\n496: \n497: read mode-registry.json and hub-router.json\n498: classify the request to one or more workflowMode values using hub-router.json\n499:   (dominant authoring/quality intent; a command like /create:agent resolves directly)\n500: \n501: if confidence is low, intent is contradictory, or routerPolicy.defaultMode is null and no mode wins:\n502:   for resource in hub_router.routerPolicy.defaultResource:   # the ordered defer set, never hardcoded here\n503:     load_if_available(resource, seen)\n504:   return UNKNOWN_FALLBACK with disambiguation_checklist = UNKNOWN_FALLBACK_CHECKLIST\n505: \n506: for each resolved workflowMode:\n507:   entry = the matching mode-registry.json modes[] item\n508:   if entry is missing or entry.packetKind != \"workflow\":\n509:     return defer(\"unknown sk-doc workflowMode; extend mode-registry.json and create a packet first\")\n510:   if not load_if_available(f\"{entry.packet}/SKILL.md\", seen):\n511:     return defer(\"registered packet SKILL.md is missing; repair the packet before routing\")\n512: \n513: return single or orderedBundle according to hub-router.json routerPolicy.outcomes\n514: ```\n515: `routerPolicy.defaultMode` is `null`: an unclear documentation intent asks for disambiguation rather than forcing a stale default. `hub-router.json` carries the router signals, vocabulary classes, default fallback resource, and bundle rules. Outcomes are `single`, `orderedBundle`, or `defer` — there is no `surfaceBundle` (no surface axis).\n516: \n517: Per-packet behavior is **not flattened**: each packet keeps its own authoring contract, references, assets, scripts, and templates.\n518: \n519: This hub does **not** use keyed resource discovery (`references/<key>/` or `assets/<key>/`) at the hub root.\n520: There are no hub-root `references/` or `assets/` directories, and packet resources stay inside their owning packet or the shared backbone.\n521: If a future workflow needs keyed resource subdirectories, add that behavior inside the owning packet using guarded runtime discovery.\n522: Do not hardcode resource inventories in this hub.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-doc/ROUTER.md, lines 1-522\n1: ---\n2: title: sk-doc Surface Router — per-intent leaf sets\n3: description: First-class surface router document at the sk-doc hub root (ROUTER.md). hub-router.json selects the workflow mode; this doc maps a request's documentation/authoring intent to the exact packet-local leaf resources that mode should load, emitting canonical (workflowMode, leafResourceId) pairs. Relocated verbatim from shared/references/smart-routing.md (v1.0.0.0) as the ROUTER.md consolidation pattern.\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"sk-doc surface router\"\n6:   - \"doc authoring resource map\"\n7:   - \"packet leaf resource routing\"\n8:   - \"surface router pattern\"\n9: importance_tier: important\n10: contextType: implementation\n11: version: 2.4.6.0\n12: router_state: active\n13: skill_pointer: SKILL.md\n14: ---\n15: \n16: # sk-doc Surface Router — per-intent leaf sets\n17: \n18: The machine router for [`SKILL.md`](./SKILL.md). `SKILL.md` keeps the short routing prose and the link that points here; this file holds the declaration a reader parses to decide what loads.\n19: \n20: ---\n21: \n22: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n23: \n24: This is sk-doc's second-layer (surface) router, first-class at the hub root as\n25: `ROUTER.md`. The hub selects a workflow mode in [`hub-router.json`](hub-router.json)\n26: (sk-create-skill, sk-create-skill-parent, sk-create-readme, sk-create-agent, sk-create-command,\n27: sk-create-feature-catalog, sk-create-manual-testing-playbook, sk-create-benchmark,\n28: sk-create-changelog, sk-create-diff, sk-create-repo-rule, sk-create-frontmatter,\n29: sk-create-goal, sk-create-with-human-voice, or sk-create-quality-control);\n30: this doc maps a request's documentation/authoring intent to the exact packet-local\n31: leaf resources that mode should load. Every path is either packet-qualified\n32: (`<packet>/references|assets/…`) or an authored shared-alias disk path (`shared/…`),\n33: and both convert to the canonical `(workflowMode, leafResourceId)` pair at the\n34: contract boundary (`sk-doc/sk-create-skill/scripts/lib/leaf-resource-contract.cjs`).\n35: `FULL_INVENTORY` is the single explicit full-toolkit intent — no other intent\n36: enumerates the whole hub.\n37: \n38: Routing is two stages: the hub picks the WORKFLOW mode (mode telemetry), this\n39: router picks the LEAVES within it. The two layers stay separate — the hub never\n40: emits leaf paths, and this router never re-decides the mode.\n41: \n42: ---\n43: \n44: ## 2. INTENT MODEL\n45: \n46: - **doc-quality leaves** — the validation rules, core standards, evergreen\n47:   packet-id rule, and the quality-control workflow a request to validate, audit,\n48:   or score documentation against sk-doc standards loads. Fired by \"documentation\n49:   quality / validate documentation / validate a document / score this document /\n50:   DQI / document audit / review the docs / document for clarity / audit the docs /\n51:   pass review\"\n52:   requests. The scoring phrases mirror `hub-router.json`, so a request that\n53:   reaches the quality mode at stage one also reaches its leaves at stage two.\n54: - **optimization leaves** — the optimization reference and the llms.txt templates\n55:   a request to compress, trim, or token-optimize a doc, or emit a machine-readable\n56:   index loads. Fired by \"optimize / token efficiency / llms.txt / reduce tokens /\n57:   slim down\" requests.\n58: - **skill-creation leaves** — the skill creation workflow and the skill.md /\n59:   readme / reference templates a request to scaffold or author a new skill loads.\n60:   Fired by \"create a skill / skill.md scaffold / resource_map wiring / package a\n61:   capability\" requests.\n62: - **parent-hub leaves.** The nested-packet contract, the hub-router schema and\n63:   the four parent-hub templates a request to build or rewire a parent hub loads.\n64:   Fired by \"parent hub / parent skill / mode packet / nested workflow packet /\n65:   mode-registry.json / hub-router.json\" requests, the file names spelled in full\n66:   so a bare \"hub router\" in an unrelated sentence does not fire it. This is the leaf set for the\n67:   `sk-create-skill-parent` mode, which shares the `sk-create-skill` packet but\n68:   not its leaves: a plain skill request must not pull the hub templates.\n69: - **render-ledger leaves.** The validation reference that explains the render\n70:   ledger's debt, exempt and pinned records and the doc rewrite guard, and the\n71:   maintenance workflow that runs the guard before a rewrite is committed. Fired\n72:   by \"doc rewrite guard / render ledger / render-known-debt.json / exempt from\n73:   the render checks / kept every claim\" requests. It belongs to the\n74:   `sk-create-skill` mode, which owns the ledger.\n75: - **agent-creation leaves.** The agent README and agent template a request to\n76:   scaffold an OpenCode agent loads. Fired by \"create an agent / new agent /\n77:   agent frontmatter / permission object / @analyze agent\" requests.\n78: - **command-creation leaves.** The command README and command template a\n79:   request to scaffold a slash command loads. Fired by \"create a command / slash\n80:   command / argument-hint / allowed-tools / thin router\" requests.\n81: - **agent-command leaves.** Both packets' READMEs and templates a request to\n82:   author an agent together with its paired `/create` command loads. Fired only\n83:   by the two paired phrases, \"agent and paired\" and \"paired /create\", which no\n84:   single-artifact request carries. The paired intent stays separate rather than\n85:   being expressed as the union of the two above, because the router keeps only\n86:   intents within one point of the top score: a paired request that also names\n87:   the agent would otherwise drop the command half.\n88: - **diagram and chart leaves belong to the `sk-design` hub.** ASCII flowchart,\n89:   decision-tree and standalone HTML chart authoring is `sk-design-diagram` and\n90:   `sk-design-chart` work, and this router declares no intent for it: `/design:diagram`\n91:   and `/design:chart` are the entry points, so \"flowchart\", \"ascii\", \"process diagram\",\n92:   \"create a chart\" and \"plot the data\" reach no key in the two maps below.\n93: - **human-voice leaves** — the scope gate, the scoring method and the Human\n94:   Voice Rules standard a request to apply or score that standard loads. Fired\n95:   by \"hvr / human voice rules / apply human voice / rewrite in human voice /\n96:   make this sound human / sounds ai-generated / remove ai tells\" requests. The\n97:   scope gate is the always-loaded leaf, because deciding what a voice edit may\n98:   touch comes before deciding what to change.\n99: - **playbook leaves** — the manual-testing-playbook README a request to author a\n100:   manual testing playbook loads. Fired by \"playbook system / manual testing\n101:   playbook / testing playbook / playbook scenario / manual test\n102:   scenario\" requests.\n103: - **playbook-conversation leaves** — the conversation-scenario reference and the\n104:   conversation snippet template a request to write a multi-turn json conversation script for\n105:   a playbook scenario loads. Fired by \"playbook conversation / multi-turn\n106:   conversation script / json conversation / conversation runner\" requests. This\n107:   intent scores double. \"manual testing playbook\" also matches \"testing\n108:   playbook\", so that one phrase scores the playbook intent twice, and at equal\n109:   weight a request naming both a playbook and a conversation would load only the\n110:   playbook README. At double weight the two tie and both leaf sets load.\n111: - **feature-catalog leaves** — the feature-catalog README a request to inventory a\n112:   project's features / capabilities loads. Fired by \"feature catalog / feature\n113:   inventory / capabilities\" requests.\n114: - **readme-creation leaves** — the readme README and readme template a request to\n115:   author a project README / front-page overview loads. Fired by \"create a readme /\n116:   readme for / project overview / landing doc\" requests.\n117: - **changelog leaves** — the changelog template a request to author release notes\n118:   / version notes loads. Fired by \"changelog / release notes / what shipped /\n119:   since the last version\" requests.\n120: - **benchmark leaves** — the benchmark shared README, worked example, and report\n121:   template a request to author a behavior benchmark\n122:   loads. Fired by \"create a benchmark / benchmark suite / behavior benchmark\"\n123:   requests.\n124: - **diff leaves** — the diff README, workflow, and worked example a request to\n125:   produce a before/after document diff loads. Fired by \"document diff / before\n126:   and after diff / visual document diff\" requests.\n127: - **repo-rule leaves** — the four decision tests, the rule anatomy contract, the\n128:   creation standards, the wiring contract, the rule and router templates, and the\n129:   packet reference map a\n130:   request to add, change, or remove a repo-local rule loads. Fired by \"repo rule /\n131:   project rule / REPO RULES.md / trigger table / retire a repo rule\" requests. Most such\n132:   requests end in a refusal, so `decision-tests.md` is the always-loaded leaf.\n133: - **frontmatter leaves** — the field reference and the versioning standard a\n134:   request about a frontmatter block itself loads. Fired by \"yaml frontmatter /\n135:   frontmatter block / frontmatter field / description budget / 4-part version /\n136:   frontmatter versioning\" requests. The phrases are qualified rather than bare,\n137:   because `agent frontmatter` belongs to agent-creation and a bare \"frontmatter\"\n138:   would take that route away from it. The field reference is the always-loaded\n139:   leaf, because resolving the document class comes before any field rule.\n140: - **goal-authoring leaves** — the goal authoring standards, the goal exemplars,\n141:   the parent/nested goal workflows and the budget/handoff reference a request to\n142:   author or revise a packet's `goal.md` loads. Fired by \"packet goal / goal.md /\n143:   goal document / author goal.md / phase parent goal / goal criteria /\n144:   completion checks\" requests. The phrases are qualified rather than bare,\n145:   because a bare \"goal\" and the session verbs belong to session-goal management\n146:   (set, bind, update, resend), which the goal hooks and host commands own. The\n147:   authoring standards are the always-loaded leaf, because what a goal must state\n148:   comes before how one is phrased.\n149: - **full-inventory leaves** — the entire sk-doc toolkit. `FULL_INVENTORY` is the\n150:   single explicit full-toolkit intent; no other intent enumerates the whole hub.\n151:   Fired only by an explicit \"show the full sk-doc toolkit / everything sk-doc\n152:   offers\" request.\n153: \n154: A single dominant documentation axis routes to one mode's leaf set; two clearly\n155: separate axes route to both.\n156: \n157: ---\n158: \n159: ## 3. MACHINE-READABLE ROUTER (replay / benchmark source)\n160: \n161: The single machine-readable projection of the intent model above. The prose is the\n162: human-facing contract; this block is the byte-for-byte source the deterministic\n163: router-replay parses. Keep them in sync: when a map row changes above, update the\n164: matching `RESOURCE_MAP` entry here. Every `RESOURCE_MAP` path resolves on disk and\n165: dual-reads to a canonical typed pair through `leaf-manifest.json` or the hub's\n166: `leaf-aliases.json`.\n167: \n168: ```python\n169: INTENT_SIGNALS = {\n170:     \"DOC_QUALITY\": {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"documentation quality\", \"doc quality\", \"validate documentation\", \"validate a document\", \"validate this document\", \"validate markdown\", \"validation rules\", \"score this document\", \"score this doc\", \"dqi\", \"document audit\", \"fail sk-doc standards\", \"review the docs\", \"review the documentation\", \"document for clarity\", \"check the docs\", \"check the documentation\", \"audit the docs\", \"documentation review bar\", \"quality bar\", \"flag\", \"meet our standards\", \"audit documentation quality\", \"ai-friendly documentation\", \"extract structure\", \"validate\", \"score\", \"harden\", \"audit\", \"/doc:quality\"]},\n171:     \"OPTIMIZATION\": {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"optimize\", \"token efficiency\", \"llms.txt\", \"llmstxt\", \"reduce tokens\", \"fewer tokens\", \"trim\", \"compress the doc\", \"slim down\", \"model's budget\", \"machine-readable index\", \"optimize this doc\"]},\n172:     \"SKILL_CREATION\": {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"sk-skill\", \"create a new sk\", \"create sk-\", \"skill.md scaffold\", \"skill.md and starter\", \"resource_map wiring\", \"new skill\", \"create a skill\", \"build a skill\", \"author a skill\", \"scaffold a skill\", \"reusable capability\", \"reusable helper\", \"starter reference docs\", \"new capability\", \"capability module\", \"package a capability\", \"create skill\", \"skill scaffold\", \"opencode skill\", \"package skill\", \"which json files does a skill need\", \"skill root metadata contract\", \"leaf-manifest config aliases required\", \"/create:skill\"]},\n173:     \"PARENT_HUB\": {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"parent hub\", \"parent skill\", \"mode packet\", \"mode packets\", \"nested packet\", \"nested workflow packet\", \"mode-registry.json\", \"hub-router.json\", \"hub versus standalone skill class\", \"skill hub\", \"/create:skill-parent\"]},\n174:     \"RENDER_LEDGER\": {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"doc rewrite guard\", \"doc-rewrite-guard.py\", \"rewrite guard\", \"render ledger\", \"render-known-debt.json\", \"exempt from the render checks\", \"render exempt map\", \"kept every claim\", \"kept every original claim\"]},\n175:     \"AGENT_CREATION\": {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"create an agent\", \"create agent\", \"new agent\", \"author an agent\", \"scaffold an agent\", \"agent file\", \"agent persona\", \"agent frontmatter\", \"agent template\", \"permission object\", \"authority boundary\", \"@analyze agent\", \"opencode agent\", \"claude code agent\", \"/create:agent\"]},\n176:     \"COMMAND_CREATION\": {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"create a command\", \"create command\", \"slash command\", \"new slash command\", \"author a command\", \"scaffold a command\", \"argument-hint\", \"allowed-tools\", \"command template\", \"router presentation split\", \"thin router\", \"presentation contract\", \"opencode command\", \"$arguments\", \"/create:command\"]},\n177:     \"AGENT_COMMAND\": {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"agent and paired\", \"paired /create\"]},\n178:     \"HVR\": {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"hvr\", \"human voice rules\", \"apply human voice\", \"rewrite in human voice\", \"make this sound human\", \"sounds ai-generated\", \"reads like ai wrote it\", \"reads like a machine wrote it\", \"remove ai tells\", \"ai writing tells\", \"voice pass\", \"de-ai the writing\", \"banned word check\", \"human voice\"]},\n179:     \"PLAYBOOK\": {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"playbook system\", \"manual testing playbook\", \"testing playbook\", \"deterministic scenario\", \"evidence collection\", \"operator validation\", \"multi-agent execution\", \"release readiness\", \"playbook scenario\", \"manual test scenario\", \"/create:manual-testing-playbook\"]},\n180:     \"PLAYBOOK_CONVERSATION\": {\"weight\": 8, \"keywords\": [\"playbook conversation\", \"multi-turn conversation script\", \"json conversation\", \"conversation runner\", \"run-conversation-scenario\"]},\n181:     \"FEATURE_CATALOG\": {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"feature catalog\", \"feature inventory\", \"catalog of features\", \"features overview\", \"capabilities\", \"catalog package\", \"per-feature files\", \"source anchors\", \"root catalog\", \"capability inventory\", \"/create:feature-catalog\"]},\n182:     \"README_CREATION\": {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"create a readme\", \"readme for\", \"a readme\", \"front-page overview\", \"project overview\", \"getting started doc\", \"intro doc\", \"overview doc\", \"landing doc\", \"create readme\", \"readme\", \"folder readme\", \"write readme\", \"readme.md\", \"code folder readme\", \"/create:readme\"]},\n183:     \"CHANGELOG\": {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"changelog\", \"release notes\", \"version notes\", \"release summary\", \"what shipped\", \"since the last version\", \"changelog entry\", \"nested changelog\", \"packet changelog\", \"packet-local changelog\", \"create changelog\", \"global changelog\", \"vx.y.z.b\", \"/create:changelog\"]},\n184:     \"BENCHMARK\": {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"create a benchmark\", \"author a benchmark\", \"benchmark suite\", \"benchmark authoring\", \"behavior benchmark\", \"benchmark-report.md\", \"source.md\", \"mcp-server/benchmarks\", \"behavior-benchmark.md\", \"scenario contract\", \"claude-baseline\", \"benchmark/readme.md\", \"run-label folder\", \"benchmark package\", \"benchmark fixture\", \"benchmark profile\", \"reviewer-prompt fixture\", \"agent quality dimensions\", \"new benchmark\", \"promote benchmark folder\", \"create benchmark\", \"behavior_benchmark\"]},\n185:     \"DIFF\": {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"document diff\", \"doc diff\", \"diff document\", \"before and after diff\", \"before/after diff\", \"visual document diff\", \"create diff report\", \"document before after review\", \"before after document diff\", \"document change report\", \"before and after document\", \"document before/after review\", \"before/after document diff\", \"review document edits\", \"docx diff\", \"pdf diff\"]},\n186:     \"REPO_RULE\": {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"repo rule\", \"repo-rules\", \"repo rules\", \"project rule\", \"repo rule file\", \"REPO RULES.md\", \"trigger table\", \"rule router\", \"retire a rule\", \"retire a repo rule\", \"revise a rule\", \"revise a repo rule\", \"add a repo rule\", \"always-loaded rule\", \"rule that binds\", \"create repo rule\", \"new repo rule\", \"repo-local rule\", \"we should always\", \"stop doing\", \"add a rule\", \"/create:repo-rule\"]},\n187:     \"FRONTMATTER\": {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"yaml frontmatter\", \"frontmatter block\", \"frontmatter template\", \"frontmatter field\", \"frontmatter fields\", \"trigger_phrases\", \"trigger phrases\", \"importance_tier\", \"contextType\", \"description budget\", \"4-part version\", \"X.Y.Z.W\", \"frontmatter versioning\", \"frontmatter version\", \"version field\", \"frontmatter validation\", \"missing frontmatter\", \"frontmatter contract\", \"yaml header\", \"goes at the top of the file\", \"version number at the top\", \"importance tier\", \"versioning pass\", \"stopped showing up in suggestions\", \"validator says my file is missing\", \"description too long\", \"edit count\", \"field the validator wants\"]},\n188:     \"GOAL_AUTHORING\": {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"create packet goal\", \"packet goal\", \"goal.md\", \"author goal.md\", \"revise packet goal\", \"phase parent goal\", \"nested phase child goal\", \"nested goal\", \"add a goal file\", \"write a goal file\", \"draft a goal file\", \"goal file\", \"goal document\", \"goal criteria\", \"completion checks\", \"measurable criteria\", \"durable objective\", \"phase objective\", \"parent objective\", \"objective for a packet\", \"phase-child goal\", \"child-phase goal\", \"binding table\", \"goal chat slice\", \"/create:goal\"]},\n189:     \"FULL_INVENTORY\": {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"full sk-doc toolkit\", \"all templates\", \"entire toolkit\", \"everything sk-doc offers\"]},\n190: }\n191: \n192: RESOURCE_MAP = {\n193:     \"DOC_QUALITY\": [\n194:         \"shared/references/validation.md\",\n195:         \"sk-create-quality-control/references/workflows.md\",\n196:         \"shared/references/core-standards.md\",\n197:         \"shared/references/evergreen-packet-id-rule.md\"\n198:     ],\n199:     \"OPTIMIZATION\": [\n200:         \"sk-create-quality-control/references/optimization.md\",\n201:         \"shared/assets/llmstxt-templates.md\"\n202:     ],\n203:     \"SKILL_CREATION\": [\n204:         \"sk-create-skill/references/skill/creation-workflow.md\",\n205:         \"sk-create-skill/assets/skill/skill-md-template.md\",\n206:         \"sk-create-skill/assets/skill/skill-readme-template.md\",\n207:         \"sk-create-skill/assets/skill/skill-reference-template.md\"\n208:     ],\n209:     \"RENDER_LEDGER\": [\n210:         \"sk-create-skill/references/shared/validation-and-packaging.md\",\n211:         \"sk-create-skill/references/skill/examples-and-maintenance.md\"\n212:     ],\n213:     \"PARENT_HUB\": [\n214:         \"sk-create-skill/references/parent-skill/parent-skills-nested-packets.md\",\n215:         \"sk-create-skill/references/parent-skill/parent-hub-router-schema.md\",\n216:         \"sk-create-skill/assets/parent-skill/parent-skill-hub-template.md\",\n217:         \"sk-create-skill/assets/parent-skill/parent-skill-registry-template.json\",\n218:         \"sk-create-skill/assets/parent-skill/parent-skill-hub-router-template.json\",\n219:         \"sk-create-skill/assets/parent-skill/parent-skill-root-router-template.md\"\n220:     ],\n221:     \"AGENT_CREATION\": [\n222:         \"sk-create-agent/references/README.md\",\n223:         \"sk-create-agent/assets/agent-template.md\"\n224:     ],\n225:     \"COMMAND_CREATION\": [\n226:         \"sk-create-command/references/README.md\",\n227:         \"sk-create-command/assets/command-template.md\"\n228:     ],\n229:     \"AGENT_COMMAND\": [\n230:         \"sk-create-agent/references/README.md\",\n231:         \"sk-create-agent/assets/agent-template.md\",\n232:         \"sk-create-command/references/README.md\",\n233:         \"sk-create-command/assets/command-template.md\"\n234:     ],\n235:     \"HVR\": [\n236:         \"sk-create-with-human-voice/references/scope-and-exemptions.md\",\n237:         \"sk-create-with-human-voice/references/scoring-and-verification.md\",\n238:         \"sk-create-with-human-voice/assets/voice-report-template.md\",\n239:         \"sk-create-with-human-voice/references/hvr-rules.md\"\n240:     ],\n241:     \"PLAYBOOK\": [\n242:         \"sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/references/README.md\"\n243:     ],\n244:     \"PLAYBOOK_CONVERSATION\": [\n245:         \"sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/references/conversation-scenarios.md\",\n246:         \"sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/assets/manual-testing-playbook-conversation-snippet-template.md\"\n247:     ],\n248:     \"FEATURE_CATALOG\": [\n249:         \"sk-create-feature-catalog/references/README.md\"\n250:     ],\n251:     \"README_CREATION\": [\n252:         \"sk-create-readme/references/README.md\",\n253:         \"sk-create-readme/assets/readme-template.md\"\n254:     ],\n255:     \"CHANGELOG\": [\n256:         \"sk-create-changelog/assets/changelog-template.md\"\n257:     ],\n258:     \"BENCHMARK\": [\n259:         \"sk-create-benchmark/references/shared/README.md\",\n260:         \"sk-create-benchmark/references/shared/worked-example.md\",\n261:         \"sk-create-benchmark/assets/shared/benchmark-report-template.md\"\n262:     ],\n263:     \"DIFF\": [\n264:         \"sk-create-diff/references/README.md\",\n265:         \"sk-create-diff/references/workflow.md\",\n266:         \"sk-create-diff/references/worked-example.md\"\n267:     ],\n268:     \"REPO_RULE\": [\n269:         \"sk-create-repo-rule/references/decision-tests.md\",\n270:         \"sk-create-repo-rule/references/rule-anatomy.md\",\n271:         \"sk-create-repo-rule/references/creation-standards.md\",\n272:         \"sk-create-repo-rule/references/agents-md-integration.md\",\n273:         \"sk-create-repo-rule/assets/repo-rule-template.md\",\n274:         \"sk-create-repo-rule/assets/repo-rules-router-template.md\",\n275:         \"sk-create-repo-rule/references/README.md\"\n276:     ],\n277:     \"FRONTMATTER\": [\n278:         \"sk-create-frontmatter/assets/frontmatter-templates.md\",\n279:         \"sk-create-frontmatter/references/frontmatter-versioning.md\",\n280:         \"sk-create-frontmatter/references/README.md\"\n281:     ],\n282:     \"GOAL_AUTHORING\": [\n283:         \"sk-create-goal/references/authoring-standards.md\",\n284:         \"sk-create-goal/assets/goal-exemplars.md\",\n285:         \"sk-create-goal/references/parent-and-nested-goals.md\",\n286:         \"sk-create-goal/references/budget-and-handoff.md\",\n287:         \"sk-create-goal/assets/goal-top-level-template.md\",\n288:         \"sk-create-goal/assets/goal-phase-parent-template.md\",\n289:         \"sk-create-goal/assets/goal-phase-child-template.md\"\n290:     ],\n291:     \"FULL_INVENTORY\": [\n292:         \"sk-create-agent/assets/agent-template.md\",\n293:         \"sk-create-agent/references/README.md\",\n294:         \"sk-create-agent/references/agent-vs-skill-vs-command.md\",\n295:         \"sk-create-agent/references/common-pitfalls.md\",\n296:         \"sk-create-agent/references/permission-design.md\",\n297:         \"sk-create-frontmatter/assets/fixtures/README.md\",\n298:         \"sk-create-frontmatter/assets/fixtures/over-budget-description.md\",\n299:         \"sk-create-frontmatter/assets/fixtures/under-budget-trim-lost-tokens.md\",\n300:         \"sk-create-frontmatter/assets/frontmatter-templates.md\",\n301:         \"sk-create-frontmatter/references/README.md\",\n302:         \"sk-create-frontmatter/references/frontmatter-versioning.md\",\n303:         \"sk-create-goal/assets/goal-exemplars.md\",\n304:         \"sk-create-goal/assets/goal-phase-child-template.md\",\n305:         \"sk-create-goal/assets/goal-phase-parent-template.md\",\n306:         \"sk-create-goal/assets/goal-top-level-template.md\",\n307:         \"sk-create-goal/references/authoring-standards.md\",\n308:         \"sk-create-goal/references/budget-and-handoff.md\",\n309:         \"sk-create-goal/references/parent-and-nested-goals.md\",\n310:         \"sk-create-benchmark/assets/behavior-benchmark/behavior-benchmark-baseline-template.md\",\n311:         \"sk-create-benchmark/assets/behavior-benchmark/behavior-benchmark-index-template.md\",\n312:         \"sk-create-benchmark/assets/behavior-benchmark/behavior-benchmark-scenario-template.md\",\n313:         \"sk-create-benchmark/assets/shared/benchmark-report-template.md\",\n314:         \"sk-create-benchmark/assets/shared/source-template.md\",\n315:         \"sk-create-benchmark/references/behavior-benchmark/behavior-benchmark-guide.md\",\n316:         \"sk-create-benchmark/references/shared/README.md\",\n317:         \"sk-create-benchmark/references/shared/case-studies.md\",\n318:         \"sk-create-benchmark/references/shared/pitfalls.md\",\n319:         \"sk-create-benchmark/references/shared/worked-example.md\",\n320:         \"sk-create-changelog/assets/changelog-template.md\",\n321:         \"sk-create-changelog/references/README.md\",\n322:         \"sk-create-changelog/references/topology-edge-cases.md\",\n323:         \"sk-create-changelog/references/version-bump-rules.md\",\n324:         \"sk-create-changelog/references/worked-examples.md\",\n325:         \"sk-create-command/assets/command-contract.json\",\n326:         \"sk-create-command/assets/command-contract.schema.json\",\n327:         \"sk-create-command/assets/command-presentation-template.md\",\n328:         \"sk-create-command/assets/command-router-template.md\",\n329:         \"sk-create-command/assets/command-template.md\",\n330:         \"sk-create-command/references/README.md\",\n331:         \"sk-create-command/references/argument-hints-and-modes.md\",\n332:         \"sk-create-command/references/common-pitfalls.md\",\n333:         \"sk-create-command/references/router-presentation-split.md\",\n334:         \"sk-create-command/references/worked-example.md\",\n335:         \"sk-create-diff/assets/fixtures/README.md\",\n336:         \"sk-create-diff/assets/fixtures/onboarding-after.md\",\n337:         \"sk-create-diff/assets/fixtures/onboarding-before.md\",\n338:         \"sk-create-diff/references/README.md\",\n339:         \"sk-create-diff/references/accessibility-contract.md\",\n340:         \"sk-create-diff/references/capabilities-and-fidelity.md\",\n341:         \"sk-create-diff/references/cli-reference.md\",\n342:         \"sk-create-diff/references/worked-example.md\",\n343:         \"sk-create-diff/references/workflow.md\",\n344:         \"sk-create-feature-catalog/assets/feature-catalog-snippet-template.md\",\n345:         \"sk-create-feature-catalog/assets/feature-catalog-template.md\",\n346:         \"sk-create-feature-catalog/references/README.md\",\n347:         \"sk-create-feature-catalog/references/common-pitfalls.md\",\n348:         \"sk-create-feature-catalog/references/examples.md\",\n349:         \"sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/assets/manual-testing-playbook-conversation-snippet-template.md\",\n350:         \"sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/assets/manual-testing-playbook-snippet-template.md\",\n351:         \"sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/assets/manual-testing-playbook-template.md\",\n352:         \"sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/references/README.md\",\n353:         \"sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/references/common-pitfalls.md\",\n354:         \"sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/references/conversation-scenarios.md\",\n355:         \"sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/references/examples.md\",\n356:         \"sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/references/prompt-voice.md\",\n357:         \"shared/assets/llmstxt-templates.md\",\n358:         \"shared/references/core-standards.md\",\n359:         \"shared/references/evergreen-packet-id-rule.md\",\n360:         \"shared/references/validation.md\",\n361:         \"sk-create-quality-control/references/README.md\",\n362:         \"sk-create-quality-control/references/optimization.md\",\n363:         \"sk-create-quality-control/references/transformation-patterns.md\",\n364:         \"sk-create-quality-control/references/validation-and-enforcement.md\",\n365:         \"sk-create-quality-control/references/workflow-examples.md\",\n366:         \"sk-create-quality-control/references/workflows.md\",\n367:         \"sk-create-readme/assets/architecture-template.md\",\n368:         \"sk-create-readme/assets/readme-code-template.md\",\n369:         \"sk-create-readme/assets/readme-template.md\",\n370:         \"sk-create-readme/references/README.md\",\n371:         \"sk-create-readme/references/readme/quality-and-checklist.md\",\n372:         \"sk-create-readme/references/readme/types-and-voice.md\",\n373:         \"sk-create-readme/references/readme/writing-patterns.md\",\n374:         \"sk-create-repo-rule/assets/repo-rule-template.md\",\n375:         \"sk-create-repo-rule/assets/repo-rules-router-template.md\",\n376:         \"sk-create-repo-rule/references/README.md\",\n377:         \"sk-create-repo-rule/references/agents-md-integration.md\",\n378:         \"sk-create-repo-rule/references/creation-standards.md\",\n379:         \"sk-create-repo-rule/references/decision-tests.md\",\n380:         \"sk-create-repo-rule/references/rule-anatomy.md\",\n381:         \"sk-create-with-human-voice/assets/voice-report-template.md\",\n382:         \"sk-create-with-human-voice/references/README.md\",\n383:         \"sk-create-with-human-voice/references/hvr-rules.md\",\n384:         \"sk-create-with-human-voice/references/scope-and-exemptions.md\",\n385:         \"sk-create-with-human-voice/references/scoring-and-verification.md\",\n386:         \"sk-create-skill/assets/parent-skill/parent-skill-command-metadata-template.json\",\n387:         \"sk-create-skill/assets/parent-skill/parent-skill-description-template.json\",\n388:         \"sk-create-skill/assets/parent-skill/parent-skill-graph-metadata-template.json\",\n389:         \"sk-create-skill/assets/parent-skill/parent-skill-hub-router-template.json\",\n390:         \"sk-create-skill/assets/parent-skill/parent-skill-hub-template.md\",\n391:         \"sk-create-skill/assets/parent-skill/parent-skill-leaf-aliases-template.json\",\n392:         \"sk-create-skill/assets/parent-skill/parent-skill-readme-template.md\",\n393:         \"sk-create-skill/assets/parent-skill/parent-skill-registry-template.json\",\n394:         \"sk-create-skill/assets/parent-skill/parent-skill-root-router-template.md\",\n395:         \"sk-create-skill/assets/parent-skill/scaffold/hub-skill-scaffold.md\",\n396:         \"sk-create-skill/assets/parent-skill/scaffold/packet-skill-scaffold.md\",\n397:         \"sk-create-skill/assets/skill/skill-asset-template.md\",\n398:         \"sk-create-skill/assets/skill/skill-graph-metadata-template.json\",\n399:         \"sk-create-skill/assets/skill/skill-leaf-manifest-config-template.json\",\n400:         \"sk-create-skill/assets/skill/skill-md-template.md\",\n401:         \"sk-create-skill/assets/skill/skill-procedure-template.md\",\n402:         \"sk-create-skill/assets/skill/skill-readme-template.md\",\n403:         \"sk-create-skill/assets/skill/skill-reference-template.md\",\n404:         \"sk-create-skill/assets/skill/skill-scaffold-template.md\",\n405:         \"sk-create-skill/assets/skill/skill-smart-router.md\",\n406:         \"sk-create-skill/assets/skill/skill-sync-manifest-template.md\",\n407:         \"sk-create-skill/references/README.md\",\n408:         \"sk-create-skill/references/parent-skill/compiled-routing-architecture.md\",\n409:         \"sk-create-skill/references/parent-skill/compiled-routing-lockstep-surfaces.json\",\n410:         \"sk-create-skill/references/parent-skill/parent-hub-router-schema.md\",\n411:         \"sk-create-skill/references/parent-skill/parent-skills-nested-packets.md\",\n412:         \"sk-create-skill/references/shared/advisor-index-handoff.md\",\n413:         \"sk-create-skill/references/shared/common-pitfalls.md\",\n414:         \"sk-create-skill/references/shared/overview.md\",\n415:         \"sk-create-skill/references/shared/skill-root-metadata-contract.md\",\n416:         \"sk-create-skill/references/shared/validation-and-packaging.md\",\n417:         \"sk-create-skill/references/skill/creation-workflow.md\",\n418:         \"sk-create-skill/references/skill/examples-and-maintenance.md\",\n419:         \"sk-create-skill/references/skill/upgrading-a-skill-to-v4.md\"\n420:     ],\n421: }\n422: ```\n423: \n424: ---\n425: \n426: ## 4. HOW TO READ THIS\n427: \n428: - One dominant intent routes to one mode's leaf set.\n429: - Two near-tied intents (within the ambiguity delta) route to both leaf sets; the\n430:   union is deduped by canonical pair.\n431: - No keyword match is UNKNOWN_FALLBACK: confirm the target artifact and intent\n432:   before loading anything.\n433: - `FULL_INVENTORY` fires only on an explicit \"show the whole toolkit\" request.\n434: - A mode whose `mode-registry.json` row names a `command` has two whole entries: read that command's document in full and follow it under the policy the advisor names, or follow this mode's packet. Pick one and keep to it. A mode whose row has `command: null` has only its packet.\n435: \n436: ---\n437: \n438: ## 5. MACHINE ROUTER\n439: \n440: The hub's detailed routing prose, moved here from `SKILL.md` so the skill body keeps only a short routing summary and a pointer to this file. It sits last, after the stage-two map above, and it declares no map of its own: the `INTENT_SIGNALS` and `RESOURCE_MAP` block in section 3 stays the only replay source.\n441: \n442: > **Compiled routing (default-on, flag-gated, additive).** Resolve the mode via the compiled router contract first:\n443: > ```bash\n444: > node .skilled/bin/compiled-route.cjs --hub sk-doc --prompt \"<task>\"\n445: > ```\n446: > Follow the returned decision — `route` (use its `targets`), `clarify`/`defer` (disambiguate), `reject` (refuse). On a `{\"servingAuthority\":\"legacy\"}` sentinel or any error, use the routing below. The front door self-gates on serving-authority. Compiled routing is now the default for `sk-doc`; set `SPECKIT_COMPILED_ROUTING=0` to force legacy routing fleet-wide — the explicit kill-switch.\n447: \n448: ### Surface Router — per-intent leaf sets\n449: \n450: Stage 2 of routing lives in section 3 of this file.\n451: It defines three things:\n452: - The per-intent leaf model: document quality, optimization, skill creation, parent-hub creation, agent creation, command creation, the paired agent-plus-command lane, the human-voice pass, playbooks, playbook conversations, feature catalogs, READMEs, changelogs, benchmarks, diffs, frontmatter, packet goals, repo rules, and the explicit full-toolkit intent.\n453: - The machine-readable `INTENT_SIGNALS` / `RESOURCE_MAP` block that the deterministic router-replay and benchmarks parse.\n454: - The how-to-read rules: a dominant intent gives one leaf set, near-tied intents give a deduped union, and no keyword match gives the hub UNKNOWN fallback, never a silent default.\n455: \n456: Every `RESOURCE_MAP` path is packet-qualified or an authored shared-alias disk path, and each converts to the canonical `(workflowMode, leafResourceId)` pair at the one contract boundary.\n457: \n458: The reason this file stays a separate document from `SKILL.md` and from `hub-router.json` is in [the parent-skill reference](sk-create-skill/references/parent-skill/parent-skills-nested-packets.md#why-the-leaf-router-stays-in-routermd).\n459: \n460: ### The discriminator\n461: - **`workflowMode`** — the public packet key (e.g. `sk-create-skill`, `sk-create-quality-control`). `sk-create-skill-parent` is a second mode over the same `sk-create-skill` packet.\n462: - **`packetKind`** — `workflow` for every sk-doc packet (there is no surface axis; the sk-create-quality-control pipeline is universal doctrine in `shared/`, not orthogonal stack-evidence).\n463: - **`backendKind`** — `template-scaffold` for the create-* generators, `sk-create-quality-control` for the sk-create-quality-control mode.\n464: \n465: ### Routing rule\n466: ```\n467: SKILL_ROOT = path containing this ROUTER.md and its SKILL.md\n468: REGISTRY = SKILL_ROOT / \"mode-registry.json\"\n469: HUB_ROUTER = SKILL_ROOT / \"hub-router.json\"\n470: \n471: UNKNOWN_FALLBACK_CHECKLIST = [\n472:     \"Confirm whether this is sk-create-skill, sk-create-skill-parent, sk-create-readme, sk-create-agent, sk-create-command, sk-create-feature-catalog, sk-create-manual-testing-playbook, sk-create-benchmark, sk-create-changelog, sk-create-diff, sk-create-repo-rule, sk-create-frontmatter, sk-create-goal, sk-create-with-human-voice, or sk-create-quality-control work\",\n473:     \"Confirm the target document or component being authored, scaffolded, or reviewed\",\n474:     \"Provide the available inputs: existing doc, source material, or scaffold parameters\",\n475:     \"Confirm the validation expectations before completion (DQI/HVR scoring, validate.sh, or packet-specific checks)\",\n476: ]\n477: \n478: def _guard_in_skill(relative_path):\n479:   resolved = (SKILL_ROOT / relative_path).resolve()\n480:   resolved.relative_to(SKILL_ROOT)\n481:   if resolved.suffix.lower() not in {\".md\", \".json\"}:\n482:     raise ValueError(\"only skill-local markdown/json router resources are routable\")\n483:   return resolved.relative_to(SKILL_ROOT).as_posix()\n484: \n485: def load_if_available(relative_path, seen):\n486:   guarded = _guard_in_skill(relative_path)\n487:   if guarded not in seen and (SKILL_ROOT / guarded).exists():\n488:     load(guarded)\n489:     seen.add(guarded)\n490:     return True\n491:   return False\n492: \n493: seen = set()\n494: if not REGISTRY.exists() or not HUB_ROUTER.exists():\n495:   return defer(\"router metadata missing; inspect sk-doc/mode-registry.json and sk-doc/hub-router.json\")\n496: \n497: read mode-registry.json and hub-router.json\n498: classify the request to one or more workflowMode values using hub-router.json\n499:   (dominant authoring/quality intent; a command like /create:agent resolves directly)\n500: \n501: if confidence is low, intent is contradictory, or routerPolicy.defaultMode is null and no mode wins:\n502:   for resource in hub_router.routerPolicy.defaultResource:   # the ordered defer set, never hardcoded here\n503:     load_if_available(resource, seen)\n504:   return UNKNOWN_FALLBACK with disambiguation_checklist = UNKNOWN_FALLBACK_CHECKLIST\n505: \n506: for each resolved workflowMode:\n507:   entry = the matching mode-registry.json modes[] item\n508:   if entry is missing or entry.packetKind != \"workflow\":\n509:     return defer(\"unknown sk-doc workflowMode; extend mode-registry.json and create a packet first\")\n510:   if not load_if_available(f\"{entry.packet}/SKILL.md\", seen):\n511:     return defer(\"registered packet SKILL.md is missing; repair the packet before routing\")\n512: \n513: return single or orderedBundle according to hub-router.json routerPolicy.outcomes\n514: ```\n515: `routerPolicy.defaultMode` is `null`: an unclear documentation intent asks for disambiguation rather than forcing a stale default. `hub-router.json` carries the router signals, vocabulary classes, default fallback resource, and bundle rules. Outcomes are `single`, `orderedBundle`, or `defer` — there is no `surfaceBundle` (no surface axis).\n516: \n517: Per-packet behavior is **not flattened**: each packet keeps its own authoring contract, references, assets, scripts, and templates.\n518: \n519: This hub does **not** use keyed resource discovery (`references/<key>/` or `assets/<key>/`) at the hub root.\n520: There are no hub-root `references/` or `assets/` directories, and packet resources stay inside their owning packet or the shared backbone.\n521: If a future workflow needs keyed resource subdirectories, add that behavior inside the owning packet using guarded runtime discovery.\n522: Do not hardcode resource inventories in this hub."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-doc/ROUTER.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794543680879,"end":1794543681231}}}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1794543682111,"sessionID":"ses_e2cdd3a02ffe7tBn2LFIz7jxoD","part":{"id":"prt_1d323823f001tg6AGabml0dlae","sessionID":"ses_e2cdd3a02ffe7tBn2LFIz7jxoD","messageID":"msg_1d32368fe001BUxSiUjJmca3rM","type":"step-finish","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"61f79ca9caf6d618a3a2096881611c74203a15e0","cost":0,"tokens":{"input":3363,"output":126,"reasoning":66,"cache":{"read":31488,"write":0}}}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794543686008,"sessionID":"ses_e2cdd3a02ffe7tBn2LFIz7jxoD","part":{"id":"prt_1d3239178001jzd4tFd6T3PSrq","sessionID":"ses_e2cdd3a02ffe7tBn2LFIz7jxoD","messageID":"msg_1d3238243001woT83anJV1fPt0","type":"step-start","snapshot":"48dae58ff8bf04494777f78208fac1661f945a16"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794543692296,"sessionID":"ses_e2cdd3a02ffe7tBn2LFIz7jxoD","part":{"partID":"prt_1d323a5f2001iiuhJn9vo3LqHz","sessionID":"ses_e2cdd3a02ffe7tBn2LFIz7jxoD","messageID":"msg_1d3238243001woT83anJV1fPt0","type":"tool","id":"call_544ac9986aaf4b51a8f97cd8","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md, lines 1-245\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Communication\"\n3: description: \"Write so the reader can act after one pass: no table in a reply, nothing that does not carry information.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"cut filler\"\n6:   - \"empty opener\"\n7:   - \"match length to the question\"\n8:   - \"no tables\"\n9:   - \"can you say that more plainly\"\n10:   - \"I don't follow\"\n11:   - \"too abstract\"\n12:   - \"number the steps\"\n13:   - \"item cap\"\n14:   - \"reply too long\"\n15:   - \"say each fact once\"\n16:   - \"one idea per sentence\"\n17:   - \"plain words\"\n18:   - \"em dash\"\n19:   - \"reads like terminal output\"\n20:   - \"concise is not compressed\"\n21:   - \"recommend one option\"\n22:   - \"buried the verdict\"\n23:   - \"say where you are going\"\n24:   - \"time estimate\"\n25: importance_tier: important\n26: contextType: reference\n27: version: 1.5.0.0\n28: ---\n29: \n30: # Rule: Communication\n31: \n32: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before writing any substantive reply.\n33: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n34: \n35: ## Fires when\n36: \n37: - About to write any substantive reply, an answer, an explanation, a close-out, a status.\n38: - Drafting or editing a sentence a reader will read.\n39: - About to present a recommendation, a fork, or a trade-off.\n40: - About to start a multi-step stretch of work the reader will not see inside.\n41: - About to report what a long autonomous run found.\n42: - The reader says they did not follow or asks for a plainer version.\n43: \n44: ## The rule\n45: \n46: **Write so the reader can act after one pass: the answer first, and nothing in the reply\n47: that does not carry information.**\n48: \n49: ---\n50: \n51: ## 1. THE REGISTER YOU ARE IN\n52: \n53: **While working: clipped.** Act rather than narrate. Open with the result, not with\n54: \"I'll now\" or \"Let me\". Batch the tool calls and report at checkpoints.\n55: \n56: **At a boundary: dense.** You are at a boundary whenever the reader is about to decide\n57: something, act on something or take the work over: a handoff, a close-out, a decision\n58: point. Verdict first, then the receipts. Reason about the problem, not about yourself.\n59: Everything else is working.\n60: \n61: **Complex topic, simple words.** When the subject is technical or layered, explain it plainly the first time, not only after the reader asks: what it is, why it matters to them, what they do next. Use a term only when the reader needs it, and define it on first use. Simpler words, same claim: never drop a caveat or a number to get there.\n62: \n63: The failure this prevents: the wrong register. Narration nobody reads, the verdict buried\n64: in working notes or the working notes inflated into a report.\n65: \n66: ---\n67: \n68: ## 2. LENGTH\n69: \n70: **Match length to the question.** Length is earned by the reader's need, never by the work you did to get there.\n71: \n72: **Choose by reader impact before you write.** Decide who will read this and what they already hold. Keep what the reader would notice, act on or\n73: decide differently because of. Merge the items that have the same effect for them. How the\n74: work was done stays out unless it changes how far the reader can trust the result.\n75: \n76: **Concise is not compressed.** Concise cuts what the reader does not need and stops. Compressed cuts further and takes the connective tissue with it: the because, the second half of a contrast, the noun that a later \"this\" points back to. After a cut, check the joints. Every cut this file asks for answers to that floor.\n77: \n78: The failure this prevents: the answer is in there, and they did not find it, under a\n79: record of the work they never needed. Or the cut went too far, and the reader rebuilds the\n80: argument the reply no longer states.\n81: \n82: **No tables in a reply,** except the in-flight block in\n83: [`communication-handoff.md`](communication-handoff.md) §6. Use a sentence for one or two\n84: facts and bullets for parallel items.\n85: \n86: The failure this prevents: the reader parses a grid to learn what one sentence would\n87: have said.\n88: \n89: ---\n90: \n91: ## 3. CUT FILLER\n92: \n93: Every sentence carries information. The recurring offenders:\n94: \n95: - **Empty openers:** \"Great question\", \"Let me take a look\", \"I'll now\".\n96: - **Restated summaries:** repeating back what you just said, one abstraction level up.\n97: - **Vague warnings:** \"be careful with this\", \"this can be tricky\", naming no failure. A caveat earns its place by naming a failure that can happen here.\n98: - **Corporate and marketing register:** \"robust\", \"seamless\", \"leverage\", \"best-in-class\".\n99: - **Narrating the obvious:** announcing a tool call the reader can see the result of.\n100: - **Leaked scaffolding:** a runtime line that tells you to plan privately, list what you\n101:   need next or batch your calls is answered in reasoning, never in the reply.\n102: \n103: **Say each fact once.** Each part of the reply has one job: the first lines carry the\n104: outcome, the body carries the detail, and the end carries the receipts and the handback. A\n105: sentence that repeats an earlier one goes, or it says something the reader does not know\n106: yet.\n107: \n108: The failure this prevents: filler trains the reader to skim, and then they skim the\n109: sentence that mattered.\n110: \n111: ---\n112: \n113: ## 4. THE ANSWER FIRST\n114: \n115: **The first line carries the payload.** The answer, the verdict or the action, in the\n116: first sentence, not a label for it. Test: read the first line on its own. If it told you the outcome, it did its job. Four habits break it: announcing (\"I will now\n117: check the tests\"), labelling (\"Overview:\"), the fragment opener (\"Context.\") and the set-up\n118: that promises the good part in a moment.\n119: \n120: **The rest of the outcome follows at once.** What the work concluded, what changed and what\n121: to do next are in the reader's hands by the second line. The receipts come after that, and\n122: the handback comes last, per [`communication-handoff.md`](communication-handoff.md) §1.\n123: End when the answer is done. The closing-deletion test: a last line that only asks whether\n124: anything else is needed, or recaps what the reply just said, deletes.\n125: \n126: **Reach the verdict by analysis, then put it first.** The order of the reply is not the order of the thinking. If you cannot state the verdict yet, say that. A named uncertainty is a verdict about the state of the evidence.\n127: \n128: **Recommend one path.** Name its main trade-off, and mention an alternative only when it could change the decision. Mark must-do work apart from nice-to-have. State an assumption you made where evidence was missing, because the reader can correct a visible assumption and cannot correct a silent one.\n129: \n130: **An ambiguous request still gets the answer first.** When two readings lead to different answers, answer the likelier one and say in one line which reading you took. When they lead to materially different work, ask before starting it, per [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](uncertainty-and-honesty.md) §1. When you deliver a different version of the ask, the first lines say what changed and why.\n131: \n132: The failure this prevents: a payload that waits behind an announcement or a label, a survey\n133: of options where one recommendation was owed, a swapped or misread request nobody catches,\n134: or a last line that is a farewell and adds nothing.\n135: \n136: ---\n137: \n138: ## 5. HOW THE REPLY MOVES\n139: \n140: **Each paragraph stands alone and carries the reader forward.** A reader who lands mid-reply can act on the paragraph in front of them, and each one picks up where the last one landed: what changed, what it implies, what comes next.\n141: \n142: **Number the steps when there is more than one.** Anything the reader must run, check or\n143: answer gets its own number, one step per line, the number at the start of the line. Numbers\n144: bracketed inside a sentence, (1) then (2) then (3), are a paragraph wearing numbers.\n145: \n146: **No group shows more than five items.** Split a longer set into labelled groups of five or\n147: fewer, or show the five that matter most and say in one line how many are held back, \"the\n148: first five of nine\". The rest is retained, not discarded, and appears when the reader asks.\n149: \n150: **Suppress the tangent.** The reply answers the question it was asked. A second issue worth\n151: raising gets offered once, at the end, in one line. The reader decides whether it happens.\n152: \n153: The failure this prevents: the reader assembles the order themselves, loses track of which\n154: step they have done, mistakes a shortened list for the complete one, or waits for the\n155: answer behind a second issue.\n156: \n157: ---\n158: \n159: ## 6. SENTENCES AND WORDS\n160: \n161: **One idea per sentence.** Short and declarative. When a sentence stacks clauses, split it. Vary the rhythm, and prefer prose when a list would fragment a single argument.\n162: \n163: **State the relation between sentences.** A sentence says how it attaches to the one before: because, but, therefore, for example. When it explains how something works, it names the moving parts: what acts, what it acts on, what changes.\n164: \n165: **Plain words by default.** Reserve exact names for the things that have them: languages, frameworks, APIs, dependencies, commands. Introduce unavoidable jargon one term at a time, in a sentence that defines it by use.\n166: \n167: **No terse machine register.** Replace arrows and symbols standing in for verbs with action words. For stacked noun phrases, say who does what, using the [HVR nominalization guidance](../skills/sk-doc/sk-create-with-human-voice/references/hvr-rules.md#nominalization-and-stacked-compression). Explain each ID, abbreviation or status code in plain words, and say what a bare count or label measures. Turn fragments into complete sentences.\n168: \n169: **Name an identifier only where the reader acts on it.** A path, hash, flag or ID earns its place when the reader will run, open or check it. Elsewhere, say what the thing is. Keep to one or two numbers a sentence, and when the numbers are the point, give each its own sentence or bullet.\n170: \n171: The failure this prevents: a technically correct reply the reader has to decode, where\n172: they supply a link the writer never stated and cannot find the few names and figures they\n173: need.\n174: \n175: ---\n176: \n177: ## 7. PUNCTUATION THE READER TRIPS ON\n178: \n179: - **Never use an em dash.** Use a comma, a full stop or a colon, whichever the sentence wanted.\n180: - **No semicolon.** Two sentences, or a conjunction.\n181: - **No serial comma.** Drop it before the `and` or `or` that closes a list.\n182: \n183: The failure this prevents: dashes read as authored voice to a human and as a tell to a\n184: reader who has seen a lot of generated text.\n185: \n186: The full standard is the Human Voice Rules, which `sk-doc` routes to. Load all of it when writing a document. A reply takes its voice half, the directives, vocabulary lists and tell lists, and leaves its document-structure half.\n187: \n188: ---\n189: \n190: ## 8. LONG STRETCHES AND WHAT THEY FOUND\n191: \n192: **Say where you are going before a long stretch.** Before work the reader cannot see inside, post the intended path: a short numbered list of what you will do and what to expect at each checkpoint. Update it when it changes. Clipped means not narrating each step. It never means starting without saying where you are going.\n193: \n194: **Attach a concrete time estimate when the stretch is long.** Minutes or hours, based on a named earlier run, or marked as an assumption. An estimate informs and never gates. Outside that case, do not open a task with one nobody asked for, and never use one to argue against starting. When the task is too big for one pass, name the smallest slice worth delivering, start there and say what is deferred.\n195: \n196: **A synthesis is not a file path.** When a long run produces findings, the message carries the answer, the few findings behind it, the uncertainty that matters and what was ruled out. The artifact path follows them. Per-iteration narration and internal metrics stay out. Where a run has its own completion contract, as the deep-loop modes do, that contract wins.\n197: \n198: The failure this prevents: twenty minutes of silence, then a result the reader has to\n199: reverse-engineer a plan from, or a path and a count handed over in place of a report.\n200: \n201: ---\n202: \n203: ## 9. WHEN THE READER DID NOT FOLLOW\n204: \n205: \"I don't follow\", \"what?\" or \"too abstract\" calls for a change of modality: a concrete\n206: example, a numbered sequence, a smaller first step or a picture using the runtime's visual\n207: capability. Do not repeat the same explanation at greater length.\n208: \n209: \"say that more plainly\", \"in simple terms\" or \"rewrite that\" calls for a plain\n210: re-render: a copy edit that rewords without reordering, cutting or adding. Every claim,\n211: number, caveat and conclusion keeps its strength. Code, commands, paths, identifiers,\n212: quotations and numbers stay byte-exact. The wording standard is\n213: [hvr-rules.md](../skills/sk-doc/sk-create-with-human-voice/references/hvr-rules.md). If a\n214: plainer word would change what a sentence claims, keep the original.\n215: \n216: The failure this prevents: the same explanation repeats, or the plain rewrite changes the claim.\n217: \n218: ---\n219: \n220: ## 10. WHAT THIS RULE IS NOT\n221: \n222: - **Not a constraint on rigor.** Nothing here softens a claim, a caveat, or a verification standard owned by [`evidence-and-proof.md`](evidence-and-proof.md) or [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](uncertainty-and-honesty.md).\n223: - **Not a voice to perform.** When honoring a rule here would weaken the answer, keep the answer.\n224: - **Not a license to omit.** \"Match length to the question\" is about the reader's need,\n225:   never about leaving out what they have to know.\n226: - **Not licence to skip the analysis.** Verdict first is an ordering of the writing, never\n227:   a shortcut in the reasoning.\n228: \n229: ---\n230: \n231: ## 11. SELF-CHECK\n232: \n233: - [ ] The first line carries the answer or the action, not a label, an announcement or a setup, and the outcome is complete by the second line.\n234: - [ ] I reached the verdict by analysis, recommended one path with its trade-off and stated the assumptions I made.\n235: - [ ] Where the request was ambiguous or I delivered a different version of it, the first lines say which reading I took and why.\n236: - [ ] Each paragraph stands alone and carries the reader forward, and multi-step work reads as a numbered list.\n237: - [ ] No group runs past five items, nothing was dropped to keep it under, and a tangent appears once at the end or not at all.\n238: - [ ] Every sentence carries information, each fact appears once, and no runtime instruction to plan privately was copied into the reply.\n239: - [ ] Length matches what the reader asked, no table stands in a reply, and nothing was compressed past the point where the reader has to re-expand it.\n240: - [ ] Every sentence carries one idea in plain words and says how it attaches to the one before.\n241: - [ ] Arrows, bare IDs, counts and fragments were turned into sentences, and every path, hash or flag is one the reader will act on.\n242: - [ ] No em dash, no semicolon, no serial comma.\n243: - [ ] Before a long stretch I said where I was going, with a concrete time estimate when it was long, and no estimate argued against starting.\n244: - [ ] A synthesis I reported carries its findings, not just its path and its counts.\n245: - [ ] When the reader did not follow, I changed modality, and a plain re-render kept every claim, number and caveat at the same strength.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md, lines 1-245\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Communication\"\n3: description: \"Write so the reader can act after one pass: no table in a reply, nothing that does not carry information.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"cut filler\"\n6:   - \"empty opener\"\n7:   - \"match length to the question\"\n8:   - \"no tables\"\n9:   - \"can you say that more plainly\"\n10:   - \"I don't follow\"\n11:   - \"too abstract\"\n12:   - \"number the steps\"\n13:   - \"item cap\"\n14:   - \"reply too long\"\n15:   - \"say each fact once\"\n16:   - \"one idea per sentence\"\n17:   - \"plain words\"\n18:   - \"em dash\"\n19:   - \"reads like terminal output\"\n20:   - \"concise is not compressed\"\n21:   - \"recommend one option\"\n22:   - \"buried the verdict\"\n23:   - \"say where you are going\"\n24:   - \"time estimate\"\n25: importance_tier: important\n26: contextType: reference\n27: version: 1.5.0.0\n28: ---\n29: \n30: # Rule: Communication\n31: \n32: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before writing any substantive reply.\n33: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n34: \n35: ## Fires when\n36: \n37: - About to write any substantive reply, an answer, an explanation, a close-out, a status.\n38: - Drafting or editing a sentence a reader will read.\n39: - About to present a recommendation, a fork, or a trade-off.\n40: - About to start a multi-step stretch of work the reader will not see inside.\n41: - About to report what a long autonomous run found.\n42: - The reader says they did not follow or asks for a plainer version.\n43: \n44: ## The rule\n45: \n46: **Write so the reader can act after one pass: the answer first, and nothing in the reply\n47: that does not carry information.**\n48: \n49: ---\n50: \n51: ## 1. THE REGISTER YOU ARE IN\n52: \n53: **While working: clipped.** Act rather than narrate. Open with the result, not with\n54: \"I'll now\" or \"Let me\". Batch the tool calls and report at checkpoints.\n55: \n56: **At a boundary: dense.** You are at a boundary whenever the reader is about to decide\n57: something, act on something or take the work over: a handoff, a close-out, a decision\n58: point. Verdict first, then the receipts. Reason about the problem, not about yourself.\n59: Everything else is working.\n60: \n61: **Complex topic, simple words.** When the subject is technical or layered, explain it plainly the first time, not only after the reader asks: what it is, why it matters to them, what they do next. Use a term only when the reader needs it, and define it on first use. Simpler words, same claim: never drop a caveat or a number to get there.\n62: \n63: The failure this prevents: the wrong register. Narration nobody reads, the verdict buried\n64: in working notes or the working notes inflated into a report.\n65: \n66: ---\n67: \n68: ## 2. LENGTH\n69: \n70: **Match length to the question.** Length is earned by the reader's need, never by the work you did to get there.\n71: \n72: **Choose by reader impact before you write.** Decide who will read this and what they already hold. Keep what the reader would notice, act on or\n73: decide differently because of. Merge the items that have the same effect for them. How the\n74: work was done stays out unless it changes how far the reader can trust the result.\n75: \n76: **Concise is not compressed.** Concise cuts what the reader does not need and stops. Compressed cuts further and takes the connective tissue with it: the because, the second half of a contrast, the noun that a later \"this\" points back to. After a cut, check the joints. Every cut this file asks for answers to that floor.\n77: \n78: The failure this prevents: the answer is in there, and they did not find it, under a\n79: record of the work they never needed. Or the cut went too far, and the reader rebuilds the\n80: argument the reply no longer states.\n81: \n82: **No tables in a reply,** except the in-flight block in\n83: [`communication-handoff.md`](communication-handoff.md) §6. Use a sentence for one or two\n84: facts and bullets for parallel items.\n85: \n86: The failure this prevents: the reader parses a grid to learn what one sentence would\n87: have said.\n88: \n89: ---\n90: \n91: ## 3. CUT FILLER\n92: \n93: Every sentence carries information. The recurring offenders:\n94: \n95: - **Empty openers:** \"Great question\", \"Let me take a look\", \"I'll now\".\n96: - **Restated summaries:** repeating back what you just said, one abstraction level up.\n97: - **Vague warnings:** \"be careful with this\", \"this can be tricky\", naming no failure. A caveat earns its place by naming a failure that can happen here.\n98: - **Corporate and marketing register:** \"robust\", \"seamless\", \"leverage\", \"best-in-class\".\n99: - **Narrating the obvious:** announcing a tool call the reader can see the result of.\n100: - **Leaked scaffolding:** a runtime line that tells you to plan privately, list what you\n101:   need next or batch your calls is answered in reasoning, never in the reply.\n102: \n103: **Say each fact once.** Each part of the reply has one job: the first lines carry the\n104: outcome, the body carries the detail, and the end carries the receipts and the handback. A\n105: sentence that repeats an earlier one goes, or it says something the reader does not know\n106: yet.\n107: \n108: The failure this prevents: filler trains the reader to skim, and then they skim the\n109: sentence that mattered.\n110: \n111: ---\n112: \n113: ## 4. THE ANSWER FIRST\n114: \n115: **The first line carries the payload.** The answer, the verdict or the action, in the\n116: first sentence, not a label for it. Test: read the first line on its own. If it told you the outcome, it did its job. Four habits break it: announcing (\"I will now\n117: check the tests\"), labelling (\"Overview:\"), the fragment opener (\"Context.\") and the set-up\n118: that promises the good part in a moment.\n119: \n120: **The rest of the outcome follows at once.** What the work concluded, what changed and what\n121: to do next are in the reader's hands by the second line. The receipts come after that, and\n122: the handback comes last, per [`communication-handoff.md`](communication-handoff.md) §1.\n123: End when the answer is done. The closing-deletion test: a last line that only asks whether\n124: anything else is needed, or recaps what the reply just said, deletes.\n125: \n126: **Reach the verdict by analysis, then put it first.** The order of the reply is not the order of the thinking. If you cannot state the verdict yet, say that. A named uncertainty is a verdict about the state of the evidence.\n127: \n128: **Recommend one path.** Name its main trade-off, and mention an alternative only when it could change the decision. Mark must-do work apart from nice-to-have. State an assumption you made where evidence was missing, because the reader can correct a visible assumption and cannot correct a silent one.\n129: \n130: **An ambiguous request still gets the answer first.** When two readings lead to different answers, answer the likelier one and say in one line which reading you took. When they lead to materially different work, ask before starting it, per [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](uncertainty-and-honesty.md) §1. When you deliver a different version of the ask, the first lines say what changed and why.\n131: \n132: The failure this prevents: a payload that waits behind an announcement or a label, a survey\n133: of options where one recommendation was owed, a swapped or misread request nobody catches,\n134: or a last line that is a farewell and adds nothing.\n135: \n136: ---\n137: \n138: ## 5. HOW THE REPLY MOVES\n139: \n140: **Each paragraph stands alone and carries the reader forward.** A reader who lands mid-reply can act on the paragraph in front of them, and each one picks up where the last one landed: what changed, what it implies, what comes next.\n141: \n142: **Number the steps when there is more than one.** Anything the reader must run, check or\n143: answer gets its own number, one step per line, the number at the start of the line. Numbers\n144: bracketed inside a sentence, (1) then (2) then (3), are a paragraph wearing numbers.\n145: \n146: **No group shows more than five items.** Split a longer set into labelled groups of five or\n147: fewer, or show the five that matter most and say in one line how many are held back, \"the\n148: first five of nine\". The rest is retained, not discarded, and appears when the reader asks.\n149: \n150: **Suppress the tangent.** The reply answers the question it was asked. A second issue worth\n151: raising gets offered once, at the end, in one line. The reader decides whether it happens.\n152: \n153: The failure this prevents: the reader assembles the order themselves, loses track of which\n154: step they have done, mistakes a shortened list for the complete one, or waits for the\n155: answer behind a second issue.\n156: \n157: ---\n158: \n159: ## 6. SENTENCES AND WORDS\n160: \n161: **One idea per sentence.** Short and declarative. When a sentence stacks clauses, split it. Vary the rhythm, and prefer prose when a list would fragment a single argument.\n162: \n163: **State the relation between sentences.** A sentence says how it attaches to the one before: because, but, therefore, for example. When it explains how something works, it names the moving parts: what acts, what it acts on, what changes.\n164: \n165: **Plain words by default.** Reserve exact names for the things that have them: languages, frameworks, APIs, dependencies, commands. Introduce unavoidable jargon one term at a time, in a sentence that defines it by use.\n166: \n167: **No terse machine register.** Replace arrows and symbols standing in for verbs with action words. For stacked noun phrases, say who does what, using the [HVR nominalization guidance](../skills/sk-doc/sk-create-with-human-voice/references/hvr-rules.md#nominalization-and-stacked-compression). Explain each ID, abbreviation or status code in plain words, and say what a bare count or label measures. Turn fragments into complete sentences.\n168: \n169: **Name an identifier only where the reader acts on it.** A path, hash, flag or ID earns its place when the reader will run, open or check it. Elsewhere, say what the thing is. Keep to one or two numbers a sentence, and when the numbers are the point, give each its own sentence or bullet.\n170: \n171: The failure this prevents: a technically correct reply the reader has to decode, where\n172: they supply a link the writer never stated and cannot find the few names and figures they\n173: need.\n174: \n175: ---\n176: \n177: ## 7. PUNCTUATION THE READER TRIPS ON\n178: \n179: - **Never use an em dash.** Use a comma, a full stop or a colon, whichever the sentence wanted.\n180: - **No semicolon.** Two sentences, or a conjunction.\n181: - **No serial comma.** Drop it before the `and` or `or` that closes a list.\n182: \n183: The failure this prevents: dashes read as authored voice to a human and as a tell to a\n184: reader who has seen a lot of generated text.\n185: \n186: The full standard is the Human Voice Rules, which `sk-doc` routes to. Load all of it when writing a document. A reply takes its voice half, the directives, vocabulary lists and tell lists, and leaves its document-structure half.\n187: \n188: ---\n189: \n190: ## 8. LONG STRETCHES AND WHAT THEY FOUND\n191: \n192: **Say where you are going before a long stretch.** Before work the reader cannot see inside, post the intended path: a short numbered list of what you will do and what to expect at each checkpoint. Update it when it changes. Clipped means not narrating each step. It never means starting without saying where you are going.\n193: \n194: **Attach a concrete time estimate when the stretch is long.** Minutes or hours, based on a named earlier run, or marked as an assumption. An estimate informs and never gates. Outside that case, do not open a task with one nobody asked for, and never use one to argue against starting. When the task is too big for one pass, name the smallest slice worth delivering, start there and say what is deferred.\n195: \n196: **A synthesis is not a file path.** When a long run produces findings, the message carries the answer, the few findings behind it, the uncertainty that matters and what was ruled out. The artifact path follows them. Per-iteration narration and internal metrics stay out. Where a run has its own completion contract, as the deep-loop modes do, that contract wins.\n197: \n198: The failure this prevents: twenty minutes of silence, then a result the reader has to\n199: reverse-engineer a plan from, or a path and a count handed over in place of a report.\n200: \n201: ---\n202: \n203: ## 9. WHEN THE READER DID NOT FOLLOW\n204: \n205: \"I don't follow\", \"what?\" or \"too abstract\" calls for a change of modality: a concrete\n206: example, a numbered sequence, a smaller first step or a picture using the runtime's visual\n207: capability. Do not repeat the same explanation at greater length.\n208: \n209: \"say that more plainly\", \"in simple terms\" or \"rewrite that\" calls for a plain\n210: re-render: a copy edit that rewords without reordering, cutting or adding. Every claim,\n211: number, caveat and conclusion keeps its strength. Code, commands, paths, identifiers,\n212: quotations and numbers stay byte-exact. The wording standard is\n213: [hvr-rules.md](../skills/sk-doc/sk-create-with-human-voice/references/hvr-rules.md). If a\n214: plainer word would change what a sentence claims, keep the original.\n215: \n216: The failure this prevents: the same explanation repeats, or the plain rewrite changes the claim.\n217: \n218: ---\n219: \n220: ## 10. WHAT THIS RULE IS NOT\n221: \n222: - **Not a constraint on rigor.** Nothing here softens a claim, a caveat, or a verification standard owned by [`evidence-and-proof.md`](evidence-and-proof.md) or [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](uncertainty-and-honesty.md).\n223: - **Not a voice to perform.** When honoring a rule here would weaken the answer, keep the answer.\n224: - **Not a license to omit.** \"Match length to the question\" is about the reader's need,\n225:   never about leaving out what they have to know.\n226: - **Not licence to skip the analysis.** Verdict first is an ordering of the writing, never\n227:   a shortcut in the reasoning.\n228: \n229: ---\n230: \n231: ## 11. SELF-CHECK\n232: \n233: - [ ] The first line carries the answer or the action, not a label, an announcement or a setup, and the outcome is complete by the second line.\n234: - [ ] I reached the verdict by analysis, recommended one path with its trade-off and stated the assumptions I made.\n235: - [ ] Where the request was ambiguous or I delivered a different version of it, the first lines say which reading I took and why.\n236: - [ ] Each paragraph stands alone and carries the reader forward, and multi-step work reads as a numbered list.\n237: - [ ] No group runs past five items, nothing was dropped to keep it under, and a tangent appears once at the end or not at all.\n238: - [ ] Every sentence carries information, each fact appears once, and no runtime instruction to plan privately was copied into the reply.\n239: - [ ] Length matches what the reader asked, no table stands in a reply, and nothing was compressed past the point where the reader has to re-expand it.\n240: - [ ] Every sentence carries one idea in plain words and says how it attaches to the one before.\n241: - [ ] Arrows, bare IDs, counts and fragments were turned into sentences, and every path, hash or flag is one the reader will act on.\n242: - [ ] No em dash, no semicolon, no serial comma.\n243: - [ ] Before a long stretch I said where I was going, with a concrete time estimate when it was long, and no estimate argued against starting.\n244: - [ ] A synthesis I reported carries its findings, not just its path and its counts.\n245: - [ ] When the reader did not follow, I changed modality, and a plain re-render kept every claim, number and caveat at the same strength."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794543691250,"end":1794543692296}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794543692303,"sessionID":"ses_e2cdd3a02ffe7tBn2LFIz7jxoD","part":{"partID":"prt_1d323a785001s4NSAoOf6C7HPM","sessionID":"ses_e2cdd3a02ffe7tBn2LFIz7jxoD","messageID":"msg_1d3238243001woT83anJV1fPt0","type":"tool","id":"call_761df5a50b874fbab3b4a1b5","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md, lines 1-220\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Communication handoff\"\n3: description: \"End every turn by naming what is now the operator's to do, in the form that lets them do it.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"what do i do now\"\n6:   - \"end of turn handback\"\n7:   - \"next steps for the operator\"\n8:   - \"who does what next\"\n9:   - \"the reply ended with nothing to do\"\n10:   - \"buried the ask\"\n11:   - \"i asked in prose\"\n12:   - \"structured choice instead of prose\"\n13:   - \"offer options not paragraphs\"\n14:   - \"waiting on the user\"\n15:   - \"blocked on a decision\"\n16:   - \"needs your approval\"\n17:   - \"unanswered question at the end\"\n18:   - \"should i continue\"\n19:   - \"which option do you want\"\n20:   - \"handing control back\"\n21:   - \"the operator has to act\"\n22:   - \"nothing is blocked on you\"\n23:   - \"state restatement cadence\"\n24:   - \"where were we\"\n25:   - \"catch me up\"\n26:   - \"closing contract\"\n27:   - \"one concrete next action\"\n28:   - \"what is in flight\"\n29:   - \"still running in the background\"\n30:   - \"did it stall or is it working\"\n31:   - \"will you continue on your own\"\n32:   - \"waiting on me or on a machine\"\n33:   - \"resume condition\"\n34: importance_tier: important\n35: contextType: reference\n36: version: 1.6.0.8\n37: ---\n38: \n39: # Rule: Communication handoff\n40: \n41: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before ending a turn that leaves the operator anything to do.\n42: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n43: \n44: ## Fires when\n45: \n46: - About to end a turn, of any kind, substantive or not.\n47: - About to report that work is done, blocked, partially done, or waiting on something.\n48: - About to end a turn while something you started is still running.\n49: - About to ask the operator anything.\n50: - About to state a fork, a trade-off, or two acceptable paths.\n51: - About to continue past a decision that was never actually made, which is when this rule earns its load.\n52: \n53: ## The rule\n54: \n55: **End every turn by naming what is now the operator's to do, in the form that lets them do it.**\n56: \n57: Naming is the obligation. An action the operator has to infer from a status report is an\n58: action that does not happen.\n59: \n60: ---\n61: \n62: ## 1. THE HANDBACK IS A SEPARATE THING FROM THE STATUS\n63: \n64: Every substantive turn ends with an honest status, a read-only turn included. `AGENTS.md` §10\n65: requires it and this section lists what it holds. Say each part briefly:\n66: \n67: 1. **What ran or was read, and what it returned**, with the receipts from\n68:    [`evidence-and-proof.md`](evidence-and-proof.md) §1.\n69: 2. **What is inferred** rather than observed.\n70: 3. **What only the operator can verify.**\n71: 4. **The state of the work:** edited / committed / pushed / dirty, and which branch.\n72: 5. **Known residual risk.** Any risk the operator must weigh before relying on the work, such as an untested path or a state that could not be checked. Write \"none known\" when there is none.\n73: \n74: What was not done belongs in the status as well, and [`evidence-and-proof.md`](evidence-and-proof.md) §10\n75: says how to report it. The status reports what happened. The handback reports what happens next,\n76: and it is a different document.\n77: \n78: **Restate the state when the reader must re-orient.** It fires when the direction of the\n79: work changes, when two attempts at the same fix have failed or when the work resumes after\n80: a gap. The restatement says what is done, what is open and what changed, and carries the\n81: operator's standing constraints and decisions in their own words. It replaces the previous\n82: stated state. It does not summarize the reply.\n83: \n84: The failure this prevents: a reader who returns mid task and trusts a stated state that\n85: the turns since have superseded.\n86: \n87: **Position it last**, so a reader who stops halfway through a reply has still hit it.\n88: \n89: The close of the turn is a contract with two clauses. Completed work is shown, as the\n90: changed file, the passing check or the output, after the outcome that\n91: [`communication.md`](communication.md) §4 puts first. When a command ran, name the command\n92: and its exit status or result beside the claim it supports, before your reading of it. What happens next is exactly one concrete action, in the form that lets the operator do\n93: it, or the one line that says nothing is.\n94: \n95: The failure this prevents: a close out the operator reads, agrees with and acts on\n96: nothing from, because the work was claimed instead of shown and the next steps\n97: scattered, no single concrete action among them.\n98: \n99: ---\n100: \n101: ## 2. WHAT COUNTS AS AN OPERATOR ACTION\n102: \n103: Only things the operator does, and only things that are actually theirs.\n104: \n105: | Is an operator action | Is not |\n106: |---|---|\n107: | A decision only they can make | A decision you already made and are narrating |\n108: | A credential, an approval, an access grant | A step you could take and chose not to |\n109: | Running something you cannot run | Work you left undone and are reframing as theirs |\n110: | Reviewing a change before it ships | A summary of what you just did |\n111: \n112: Padding the list with your own remaining work is the common failure: it trains the operator\n113: to skim the one list they need to read.\n114: \n115: ---\n116: \n117: ## 3. NOTHING TO DO IS ALSO AN ANSWER\n118: \n119: When nothing is blocked on the operator, say that in one line and stop.\n120: \n121: The failure this prevents: an operator who reads every reply twice looking for the thing they\n122: were supposed to notice.\n123: \n124: ---\n125: \n126: ## 4. WHEN THE THING YOU NEED IS A DECISION\n127: \n128: **Ask as a structured choice, through the question tool §5 names for your runtime, when all\n129: three hold:**\n130: \n131: - The alternatives are nameable, not open-ended.\n132: - The answer changes what you do next. A question that changes nothing is a delay.\n133: - You cannot resolve it from the request, the code or a sensible default.\n134: \n135: **Otherwise put it in prose and keep going.** `AGENTS.md` §3 already refuses \"should I\n136: continue?\" for a step that is clear and in scope.\n137: \n138: Recommend one option and say why.\n139: \n140: A lettered or numbered menu typed into the reply is not a structured choice when §5 names a\n141: tool for your runtime. It is the buried question in a different shape: the operator has to\n142: type the answer back, and nothing records it as a decision.\n143: \n144: The failure this prevents: two of them. A question nobody answers because it was a sentence in\n145: a paragraph, and a menu that appears for something the operator expected you to handle.\n146: \n147: ---\n148: \n149: ## 5. THE QUESTION SURFACE IS PER RUNTIME\n150: \n151: The posture above binds on every runtime. Resolve the surface at the runtime you are in\n152: rather than assuming one.\n153: \n154: | Runtime | Surface | How this is known |\n155: |---|---|---|\n156: | Claude Code | `AskUserQuestion`, in the main conversation only. A subagent has none | Named in this repository |\n157: | Pi | `ask_user_question` from the `@juicesharp/rpiv-ask-user-question` extension, or `question` from `pi-plan-build` | Recorded as installed in `.pi/PLUGINS.md` |\n158: | OpenCode | `question`, built in | The tool name the decision-capture sweep reads, `sweep-opencode.mjs` |\n159: | Codex | `request_user_input`, only on a turn whose tool list names it. Plan mode has it, Default mode only behind the `default_mode_request_user_input` feature, which ships off and this repository's `.codex/config.toml` turns on, and `codex exec` never | Read from the Codex 0.160.1 binary's own instructions |\n160: | Devin | `ask_user_question`. A subagent has none | `cli-devin/references/devin-tools.md` |\n161: | DSH | `ask_user_question`, in a session with an attached client. A headless run has none | `.dsh/plugins/repo-guards/README.md` |\n162: | Cursor | None | No question tool in the Cursor tool reference |\n163: | Anything else | No native surface assumed | Use the fallback below |\n164: \n165: **A session with no such surface is not exempt.** That is a runtime the table gives no tool,\n166: or a session where the tool is missing: a subagent, a headless run, `codex exec`. It falls\n167: back to a numbered list of named options with the recommendation marked, and says in one\n168: line that no question tool was available. Where the table names a tool and the session has\n169: it, the typed list is the failure §4 names, never a fallback.\n170: \n171: Do not invent a tool name to fill a gap in that table. An invented name fails silently, the\n172: question is never asked, and the turn ends looking complete. Where the row says unverified,\n173: check first or use the fallback.\n174: \n175: ---\n176: \n177: ## 6. WHAT IS STILL RUNNING, AND WHAT RESUMES YOU\n178: \n179: **Name what is in flight.** Anything you started that is still running and not yet\n180: reportable: a background agent, a dispatched lane, a watcher, a long suite, a build. One\n181: item is a line. More than one is a small table, the item and the state it is actually in.\n182: That table is this rule's one carve-out from [`communication.md`](communication.md) §2.\n183: The state is the payload: what it is doing now, how far in it is, what it is waiting on.\n184: \n185: **Say what resumes you.** One line, of the shape \"I will continue autonomously when …\",\n186: naming the event that unblocks you: a lane returning, a suite going green, a watcher firing.\n187: \n188: **Neither is ceremony.** A turn with nothing running writes no table and claims no resume\n189: condition. A turn that genuinely needs a decision says so and stops, and never claims it\n190: will continue by itself.\n191: \n192: The failure this prevents: a turn that goes quiet while work is still running, leaving the\n193: operator unable to tell whether anything is happening or whether it is their move.\n194: \n195: ---\n196: \n197: ## 7. WHAT THIS RULE IS NOT\n198: \n199: - **Not licence to ask more.** The bar in §4 is narrow on purpose, and `AGENTS.md` §2\n200:   consolidates whatever survives it into a single prompt.\n201: - **Not licence to stop early.** Naming what is left is never a substitute for finishing what\n202:   is yours, and `AGENTS.md` §3 refuses partial work framed as a checkpoint.\n203: - **Not a template.** No heading is required, no fixed wording. A reader who can act is the\n204:   only test.\n205: \n206: ---\n207: \n208: ## 8. SELF-CHECK\n209: \n210: - [ ] The status names what ran, what is inferred, what only the operator can verify, the state of the work and any known residual risk. It says \"none known\" when there is none.\n211: - [ ] The turn ends by naming what is the operator's to do, or by saying nothing is.\n212: - [ ] Every item on that list is theirs, not mine reframed.\n213: - [ ] The handback is last, and survives a reader who stops halfway.\n214: - [ ] Anything asked as a structured choice passes all three conditions in §4.\n215: - [ ] Any choice I offered carries a recommendation and a reason.\n216: - [ ] Nothing was handed back that I could have decided or done myself.\n217: - [ ] No question-tool name appears that I did not confirm for the runtime I am in.\n218: - [ ] Where the direction changed, two fixes failed or the work resumed after a gap, I restated what is done, what is open and what changed.\n219: - [ ] Anything I started that is still running is named with the state it is in, and nothing appears there that has already finished.\n220: - [ ] A claim that I will continue on my own names the event that resumes me, and no such claim stands on a turn that needs an operator decision.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md, lines 1-220\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Communication handoff\"\n3: description: \"End every turn by naming what is now the operator's to do, in the form that lets them do it.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"what do i do now\"\n6:   - \"end of turn handback\"\n7:   - \"next steps for the operator\"\n8:   - \"who does what next\"\n9:   - \"the reply ended with nothing to do\"\n10:   - \"buried the ask\"\n11:   - \"i asked in prose\"\n12:   - \"structured choice instead of prose\"\n13:   - \"offer options not paragraphs\"\n14:   - \"waiting on the user\"\n15:   - \"blocked on a decision\"\n16:   - \"needs your approval\"\n17:   - \"unanswered question at the end\"\n18:   - \"should i continue\"\n19:   - \"which option do you want\"\n20:   - \"handing control back\"\n21:   - \"the operator has to act\"\n22:   - \"nothing is blocked on you\"\n23:   - \"state restatement cadence\"\n24:   - \"where were we\"\n25:   - \"catch me up\"\n26:   - \"closing contract\"\n27:   - \"one concrete next action\"\n28:   - \"what is in flight\"\n29:   - \"still running in the background\"\n30:   - \"did it stall or is it working\"\n31:   - \"will you continue on your own\"\n32:   - \"waiting on me or on a machine\"\n33:   - \"resume condition\"\n34: importance_tier: important\n35: contextType: reference\n36: version: 1.6.0.8\n37: ---\n38: \n39: # Rule: Communication handoff\n40: \n41: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before ending a turn that leaves the operator anything to do.\n42: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n43: \n44: ## Fires when\n45: \n46: - About to end a turn, of any kind, substantive or not.\n47: - About to report that work is done, blocked, partially done, or waiting on something.\n48: - About to end a turn while something you started is still running.\n49: - About to ask the operator anything.\n50: - About to state a fork, a trade-off, or two acceptable paths.\n51: - About to continue past a decision that was never actually made, which is when this rule earns its load.\n52: \n53: ## The rule\n54: \n55: **End every turn by naming what is now the operator's to do, in the form that lets them do it.**\n56: \n57: Naming is the obligation. An action the operator has to infer from a status report is an\n58: action that does not happen.\n59: \n60: ---\n61: \n62: ## 1. THE HANDBACK IS A SEPARATE THING FROM THE STATUS\n63: \n64: Every substantive turn ends with an honest status, a read-only turn included. `AGENTS.md` §10\n65: requires it and this section lists what it holds. Say each part briefly:\n66: \n67: 1. **What ran or was read, and what it returned**, with the receipts from\n68:    [`evidence-and-proof.md`](evidence-and-proof.md) §1.\n69: 2. **What is inferred** rather than observed.\n70: 3. **What only the operator can verify.**\n71: 4. **The state of the work:** edited / committed / pushed / dirty, and which branch.\n72: 5. **Known residual risk.** Any risk the operator must weigh before relying on the work, such as an untested path or a state that could not be checked. Write \"none known\" when there is none.\n73: \n74: What was not done belongs in the status as well, and [`evidence-and-proof.md`](evidence-and-proof.md) §10\n75: says how to report it. The status reports what happened. The handback reports what happens next,\n76: and it is a different document.\n77: \n78: **Restate the state when the reader must re-orient.** It fires when the direction of the\n79: work changes, when two attempts at the same fix have failed or when the work resumes after\n80: a gap. The restatement says what is done, what is open and what changed, and carries the\n81: operator's standing constraints and decisions in their own words. It replaces the previous\n82: stated state. It does not summarize the reply.\n83: \n84: The failure this prevents: a reader who returns mid task and trusts a stated state that\n85: the turns since have superseded.\n86: \n87: **Position it last**, so a reader who stops halfway through a reply has still hit it.\n88: \n89: The close of the turn is a contract with two clauses. Completed work is shown, as the\n90: changed file, the passing check or the output, after the outcome that\n91: [`communication.md`](communication.md) §4 puts first. When a command ran, name the command\n92: and its exit status or result beside the claim it supports, before your reading of it. What happens next is exactly one concrete action, in the form that lets the operator do\n93: it, or the one line that says nothing is.\n94: \n95: The failure this prevents: a close out the operator reads, agrees with and acts on\n96: nothing from, because the work was claimed instead of shown and the next steps\n97: scattered, no single concrete action among them.\n98: \n99: ---\n100: \n101: ## 2. WHAT COUNTS AS AN OPERATOR ACTION\n102: \n103: Only things the operator does, and only things that are actually theirs.\n104: \n105: | Is an operator action | Is not |\n106: |---|---|\n107: | A decision only they can make | A decision you already made and are narrating |\n108: | A credential, an approval, an access grant | A step you could take and chose not to |\n109: | Running something you cannot run | Work you left undone and are reframing as theirs |\n110: | Reviewing a change before it ships | A summary of what you just did |\n111: \n112: Padding the list with your own remaining work is the common failure: it trains the operator\n113: to skim the one list they need to read.\n114: \n115: ---\n116: \n117: ## 3. NOTHING TO DO IS ALSO AN ANSWER\n118: \n119: When nothing is blocked on the operator, say that in one line and stop.\n120: \n121: The failure this prevents: an operator who reads every reply twice looking for the thing they\n122: were supposed to notice.\n123: \n124: ---\n125: \n126: ## 4. WHEN THE THING YOU NEED IS A DECISION\n127: \n128: **Ask as a structured choice, through the question tool §5 names for your runtime, when all\n129: three hold:**\n130: \n131: - The alternatives are nameable, not open-ended.\n132: - The answer changes what you do next. A question that changes nothing is a delay.\n133: - You cannot resolve it from the request, the code or a sensible default.\n134: \n135: **Otherwise put it in prose and keep going.** `AGENTS.md` §3 already refuses \"should I\n136: continue?\" for a step that is clear and in scope.\n137: \n138: Recommend one option and say why.\n139: \n140: A lettered or numbered menu typed into the reply is not a structured choice when §5 names a\n141: tool for your runtime. It is the buried question in a different shape: the operator has to\n142: type the answer back, and nothing records it as a decision.\n143: \n144: The failure this prevents: two of them. A question nobody answers because it was a sentence in\n145: a paragraph, and a menu that appears for something the operator expected you to handle.\n146: \n147: ---\n148: \n149: ## 5. THE QUESTION SURFACE IS PER RUNTIME\n150: \n151: The posture above binds on every runtime. Resolve the surface at the runtime you are in\n152: rather than assuming one.\n153: \n154: | Runtime | Surface | How this is known |\n155: |---|---|---|\n156: | Claude Code | `AskUserQuestion`, in the main conversation only. A subagent has none | Named in this repository |\n157: | Pi | `ask_user_question` from the `@juicesharp/rpiv-ask-user-question` extension, or `question` from `pi-plan-build` | Recorded as installed in `.pi/PLUGINS.md` |\n158: | OpenCode | `question`, built in | The tool name the decision-capture sweep reads, `sweep-opencode.mjs` |\n159: | Codex | `request_user_input`, only on a turn whose tool list names it. Plan mode has it, Default mode only behind the `default_mode_request_user_input` feature, which ships off and this repository's `.codex/config.toml` turns on, and `codex exec` never | Read from the Codex 0.160.1 binary's own instructions |\n160: | Devin | `ask_user_question`. A subagent has none | `cli-devin/references/devin-tools.md` |\n161: | DSH | `ask_user_question`, in a session with an attached client. A headless run has none | `.dsh/plugins/repo-guards/README.md` |\n162: | Cursor | None | No question tool in the Cursor tool reference |\n163: | Anything else | No native surface assumed | Use the fallback below |\n164: \n165: **A session with no such surface is not exempt.** That is a runtime the table gives no tool,\n166: or a session where the tool is missing: a subagent, a headless run, `codex exec`. It falls\n167: back to a numbered list of named options with the recommendation marked, and says in one\n168: line that no question tool was available. Where the table names a tool and the session has\n169: it, the typed list is the failure §4 names, never a fallback.\n170: \n171: Do not invent a tool name to fill a gap in that table. An invented name fails silently, the\n172: question is never asked, and the turn ends looking complete. Where the row says unverified,\n173: check first or use the fallback.\n174: \n175: ---\n176: \n177: ## 6. WHAT IS STILL RUNNING, AND WHAT RESUMES YOU\n178: \n179: **Name what is in flight.** Anything you started that is still running and not yet\n180: reportable: a background agent, a dispatched lane, a watcher, a long suite, a build. One\n181: item is a line. More than one is a small table, the item and the state it is actually in.\n182: That table is this rule's one carve-out from [`communication.md`](communication.md) §2.\n183: The state is the payload: what it is doing now, how far in it is, what it is waiting on.\n184: \n185: **Say what resumes you.** One line, of the shape \"I will continue autonomously when …\",\n186: naming the event that unblocks you: a lane returning, a suite going green, a watcher firing.\n187: \n188: **Neither is ceremony.** A turn with nothing running writes no table and claims no resume\n189: condition. A turn that genuinely needs a decision says so and stops, and never claims it\n190: will continue by itself.\n191: \n192: The failure this prevents: a turn that goes quiet while work is still running, leaving the\n193: operator unable to tell whether anything is happening or whether it is their move.\n194: \n195: ---\n196: \n197: ## 7. WHAT THIS RULE IS NOT\n198: \n199: - **Not licence to ask more.** The bar in §4 is narrow on purpose, and `AGENTS.md` §2\n200:   consolidates whatever survives it into a single prompt.\n201: - **Not licence to stop early.** Naming what is left is never a substitute for finishing what\n202:   is yours, and `AGENTS.md` §3 refuses partial work framed as a checkpoint.\n203: - **Not a template.** No heading is required, no fixed wording. A reader who can act is the\n204:   only test.\n205: \n206: ---\n207: \n208: ## 8. SELF-CHECK\n209: \n210: - [ ] The status names what ran, what is inferred, what only the operator can verify, the state of the work and any known residual risk. It says \"none known\" when there is none.\n211: - [ ] The turn ends by naming what is the operator's to do, or by saying nothing is.\n212: - [ ] Every item on that list is theirs, not mine reframed.\n213: - [ ] The handback is last, and survives a reader who stops halfway.\n214: - [ ] Anything asked as a structured choice passes all three conditions in §4.\n215: - [ ] Any choice I offered carries a recommendation and a reason.\n216: - [ ] Nothing was handed back that I could have decided or done myself.\n217: - [ ] No question-tool name appears that I did not confirm for the runtime I am in.\n218: - [ ] Where the direction changed, two fixes failed or the work resumed after a gap, I restated what is done, what is open and what changed.\n219: - [ ] Anything I started that is still running is named with the state it is in, and nothing appears there that has already finished.\n220: - [ ] A claim that I will continue on my own names the event that resumes me, and no such claim stands on a turn that needs an operator decision."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794543691653,"end":1794543692303}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794543692308,"sessionID":"ses_e2cdd3a02ffe7tBn2LFIz7jxoD","part":{"partID":"prt_1d323a8a2001A6ULaM4sYJyMuc","sessionID":"ses_e2cdd3a02ffe7tBn2LFIz7jxoD","messageID":"msg_1d3238243001woT83anJV1fPt0","type":"tool","id":"call_d9846965ed4c475dab125095","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md, lines 1-158\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Scope discipline\"\n3: description: \"The requested scope is the deliverable. Adjacent problems get named, not fixed.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"frozen scope\"\n6:   - \"scope lock\"\n7:   - \"scope drift\"\n8:   - \"out of scope\"\n9:   - \"in scope\"\n10:   - \"while I was in there\"\n11:   - \"noticed in passing\"\n12:   - \"adjacent defect\"\n13:   - \"adjacent code cleanup\"\n14:   - \"touch a file outside the ask\"\n15:   - \"rename reformat delete\"\n16:   - \"deviate from the approved plan\"\n17:   - \"amendment over absorption\"\n18:   - \"plan before acting\"\n19:   - \"what needs a yes first\"\n20:   - \"no early stop\"\n21:   - \"natural checkpoint\"\n22:   - \"good stopping point\"\n23:   - \"unrelated change\"\n24: importance_tier: important\n25: contextType: reference\n26: version: 1.0.1.2\n27: ---\n28: \n29: # Rule: Scope discipline\n30: \n31: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before touching anything you were not asked to touch.\n32: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n33: \n34: ## Fires when\n35: \n36: - You notice a defect, a smell, or a stale comment outside the files in scope.\n37: - The fix would be easier if you also changed something else.\n38: - Renaming, moving, reformatting, or deleting beyond the named area.\n39: - Deviating from an approved plan, or substituting your approach for the agreed one.\n40: - Part of the work is blocked and you are deciding what to do with the rest.\n41: \n42: ## The rule\n43: \n44: **The requested scope is the deliverable. Do not narrow it, widen it, or transform it.\n45: Adjacent problems get named, not fixed.**\n46: \n47: ---\n48: \n49: ## 1. THE THREE DRIFTS\n50: \n51: | Drift | What it looks like | Why it costs |\n52: |-------|-------------------|--------------|\n53: | **Narrowing** | Delivering the easy half and framing it as a checkpoint | The operator believes work is done that is not |\n54: | **Widening** | \"While I was in there I also…\" | Unreviewable diffs; a fix and an unrelated regression ship together |\n55: | **Transforming** | Solving the problem you find more interesting than the one asked | The stated problem is still there, and now there is new code too |\n56: \n57: Narrowing is easy to mistake for restraint. Restraint governs *how much you build*, never *how much of the ask you\n58: deliver*.\n59: \n60: ---\n61: \n62: ## 2. WHAT COUNTS AS \"EXPLICITLY IN SCOPE\"\n63: \n64: Law 2 freezes the scope; this section never widens it, it only says how to read it.\n65: Inside the frozen scope these are the *same* change, not adjacent ones:\n66: \n67: - The files named in the request, or the ones it unambiguously implies.\n68: - **Direct callers the change would break.**\n69: - The test covering the behavior you changed.\n70: - Imports, type signatures, and generated files that mechanically follow.\n71: \n72: Anything else is adjacent and goes to §4, including a caller the change does *not* break.\n73: \n74: ---\n75: \n76: ## 3. WHAT ALWAYS NEEDS A YES FIRST\n77: \n78: - Adding a dependency.\n79: - Creating a file outside the area the request named.\n80: - Deleting code you did not write in this task.\n81: - A rename or move that touches other files.\n82: - A formatting, lint, or import-ordering sweep over untouched code.\n83: - Rewriting git history, force-pushing, or touching branches and reflogs.\n84: - Anything in `blast-radius.md`'s irreversible tier.\n85: \n86: ---\n87: \n88: ## 4. THE ADJACENT-DEFECT PROTOCOL\n89: \n90: 1. **Stop.** Do not edit it.\n91: 2. **Record it** in one line: `file:line, what is wrong, why it is out of scope`.\n92: 3. **Finish the in-scope work.**\n93: 4. **Report it in close-out** as a separate list from what you changed.\n94: \n95: Fold it in only if the operator says so, or if leaving it makes the in-scope change\n96: incorrect. Then it was a caller, and §2 covers it.\n97: \n98: ---\n99: \n100: ## 5. DEVIATING FROM AN APPROVED PLAN\n101: \n102: The protocol is `AGENTS.md` §1 PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK, a hard blocker that outranks this\n103: file. Read it there. What this file adds is the adjacent case, a frozen **scope** you believe is wrong.\n104: \n105: ---\n106: \n107: ## 6. AMENDMENT OVER ABSORPTION\n108: \n109: Do not quietly build what you think was meant. Build what was specified, and in the\n110: same response say what is wrong, what you would change it to, and what it would cost.\n111: The operator decides.\n112: \n113: ---\n114: \n115: ## 7. FINISHING\n116: \n117: `AGENTS.md` §3 Execution Behavior binds: no early stop, no \"natural checkpoint\" on\n118: incomplete work, no asking permission to continue an approved, in-scope step. In scope\n119: terms:\n120: \n121: - **When one part is genuinely blocked, finish every other part in full**, then say\n122:   explicitly what you left out and why. Scaling the work down is the operator's call.\n123: - Proceeding without asking never waives a **mandatory wait:** Gate 3, a\n124:   PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK approval, the worktree-versus-branch choice, the remote-push\n125:   go-ahead, and every `blast-radius.md` stop-for-yes still block.\n126: \n127: ---\n128: \n129: ## 8. PLAN BEFORE ACTING\n130: \n131: Before the first change on any multi-step work, decide three things and\n132: say them:\n133: \n134: 1. **Which files you will read first.** Reading first is `prevent-overengineering.md` §2's\n135:    pre-write pass. *Which* files is a scope decision, because the set you read is the set\n136:    you will feel entitled to change.\n137: 2. **Which tools or commands will do the work.** Naming them catches a plan that needs\n138:    something outside the frozen scope while it is still cheap to raise.\n139: 3. **How the result will be verified.** `evidence-and-proof.md` §8 owns the proof plan.\n140:    A change with no named check has no defined edge, and an undefined edge is where\n141:    \"while I was in there\" gets in.\n142: \n143: Three sentences is a plan. The failure this prevents is not disorganization, it is the\n144: mid-task realization that the work needs a file nobody scoped, discovered at the moment\n145: when editing it feels easier than asking.\n146: \n147: ---\n148: \n149: ## 9. SELF-CHECK\n150: \n151: - [ ] Every file in my diff is in scope by §2, or has an explicit yes.\n152: - [ ] Adjacent defects are recorded and reported, not fixed.\n153: - [ ] Everything asked for is delivered, or listed as not delivered with a reason.\n154: - [ ] No formatting or cleanup noise rides along with the real change.\n155: - [ ] I named the files, the tools, and the check before the first edit.\n156: - [ ] Nothing entered the plan that was not in the frozen scope.\n157: - [ ] Every action on the always-needs-a-yes list had its yes before it happened.\n158: - [ ] Every deviation from the approved plan was stated and approved, and a plan I judged wrong was followed with the amendment proposed beside it.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md, lines 1-158\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Scope discipline\"\n3: description: \"The requested scope is the deliverable. Adjacent problems get named, not fixed.\"\n4: trigg